+{"id": "q001", "query": "What is an intelligent agent?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_140"], "ground_truth": "An intelligent agent is a computer system capable of flexible autonomous action in some environment. It is typically thought of as exhibiting three types of behaviour: reactive, pro-active, and social ability."}
+{"id": "q002", "query": "What is Agentic AI?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_25"], "ground_truth": "Agentic AI refers to AI systems that can act independently, make decisions, and perform tasks to achieve specific goals. They combine reasoning, memory, and generative abilities to plan and adapt, and unlike traditional AI they take initiative and collaborate with humans or other agents."}
+{"id": "q003", "query": "What is the contract net protocol?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_60", "chunk_61", "chunk_53"], "ground_truth": "The contract net protocol (CNP) is one of the oldest, most widely used agent interaction protocols. A manager agent announces one or several tasks, agents place bids for performing them, and the task is assigned according to an evaluation function applied to the bids (e.g. choose the cheapest agent). It is a well known task-sharing protocol for task allocation."}
+{"id": "q004", "query": "What is the blackboard system?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_72", "chunk_73"], "ground_truth": "The blackboard system is the first scheme for cooperative problem solving. The metaphor is a collection of intelligent agents gathering around a blackboard, looking at pieces of information written on it, thinking about them, and adding their conclusions. All agents can see the blackboard all the time, and multiple agents can write on it."}
+{"id": "q005", "query": "What is a real time interaction?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_160"], "ground_truth": "A real time interaction is one in which time plays a part in evaluating an agent's performance. Examples include a decision that must be made within a given time bound, a state of affairs that must occur as quickly as possible, or a task that must be repeated as often as possible."}
+{"id": "q006", "query": "What is Concurrent MetateM?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_225"], "ground_truth": "Concurrent MetateM is a multi-agent language developed by Michael Fisher. Each agent is programmed by giving it a temporal logic specification of the behavior it should exhibit, and these specifications are executed directly to generate the behavior of the agent."}
+{"id": "q007", "query": "What are the five stages of the contract net protocol?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_61", "chunk_53"], "ground_truth": "The contract net includes five stages: 1. Recognition, 2. Announcement, 3. Bidding, 4. Awarding, 5. Expediting."}
+{"id": "q008", "query": "What are the three types of behaviour exhibited by an intelligent agent?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_140"], "ground_truth": "An intelligent agent is typically thought of as exhibiting three types of behaviour: reactive, pro-active, and social ability."}
+{"id": "q009", "query": "What are the main components of a blackboard architecture?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_74"], "ground_truth": "The main components are: a blackboard (a global database containing data and partial solutions or hypotheses), a set of Knowledge Sources (KS) or agents, and a control mechanism."}
+{"id": "q010", "query": "What are the three message types allowed in AGENT0?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_220"], "ground_truth": "Messages in AGENT0 are constrained to be one of three types: Requests (to commit to action), Unrequests (to refrain from action), and Informs (which pass on information)."}
+{"id": "q011", "query": "What are the two basic performatives in FIPA ACL?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_400"], "ground_truth": "Inform and Request are the two basic performatives in FIPA ACL. Others are macro definitions defined in terms of these. Their meaning is defined by a pre-condition and a rational effect."}
+{"id": "q012", "query": "What layers does the TouringMachine control sub-system consist of?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_300"], "ground_truth": "The TouringMachine control sub-system consists of three layers: a reactive layer with situation-action rules, a planning layer for construction of plans and action selection, and a modelling layer containing symbolic representations of the mental states of other agents. The layers communicate via explicit control rules."}
+{"id": "q013", "query": "How can agents solve problems that are beyond their individual capabilities?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_45", "chunk_41"], "ground_truth": "Cooperative Distributed Problem Solving (CDPS) studies how a loosely coupled network of problem solvers can work together to solve problems beyond their individual capabilities. Problems faced by the nodes cannot be completed without cooperation, because no single node has sufficient expertise, resources, and information."}
+{"id": "q014", "query": "Why did TIT-FOR-TAT win Axelrod's tournament?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_325"], "ground_truth": "TIT-FOR-TAT won because there were other players it could co-operate with. The best approach depends heavily on what the full set of approaches is."}
+{"id": "q015", "query": "What happens when agents are assumed to act in their own interests?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_44"], "ground_truth": "If agents represent the interests of the general, we cannot make the benevolence assumption. Agents are assumed to act to further their own interests, possibly at the expense of others, which creates potential for conflict, may complicate the design task enormously, and strategic behaviour may be required."}
+{"id": "q016", "query": "What distinguishes a discrete environment from a continuous one?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_163"], "ground_truth": "An environment is discrete if there are a fixed, finite number of actions and percepts in it, otherwise it is continuous. A chess game is an example of a discrete environment, while taxi driving is continuous. Often a continuous environment is treated as discrete for simplicity."}
+{"id": "q017", "query": "How does an agent update its internal state when it receives a new percept?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_181", "chunk_180"], "ground_truth": "The agent keeps an internal data structure recording the environment state and history. The action-selection function maps internal states to actions, and an additional function next maps an internal state and a percept to a new internal state. This is how the agent updates its view of the world when it gets a new percept."}
+{"id": "q018", "query": "What does the see function output in an agent with state?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_180"], "ground_truth": "The see function is the agent's ability to observe its environment, whereas the action function represents the agent's decision-making process. The output of the see function is a percept, which maps environment states to percepts."}
+{"id": "q019", "query": "In practical reasoning agents, which function replaces the next function?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_249"], "ground_truth": "In implementing practical reasoning agents, instead of the function next (which took a percept and used it to update the internal state), there is a belief revision function."}
+{"id": "q020", "query": "What is the side effect or package deal problem in intentions?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_238"], "ground_truth": "The side effect or package deal problem: agents need not intend all the expected side effects of their intentions. I may believe that going to the dentist involves pain and intend to go, but this does not imply that I intend to suffer pain."}
+{"id": "q021", "query": "What does P{Bel} represent in implementing practical reasoning agents?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_250"], "ground_truth": "P{Bel} is the power set of beliefs, where Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have. Deliberation and means-ends reasoning are not instantaneous, so the agent selects an intention that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world."}
+{"id": "q022", "query": "Why can the benevolence assumption not be made for self-interested agents?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_44"], "ground_truth": "If agents represent the interests of the general, then we cannot make the benevolence assumption. Agents are assumed to act to further their own interests, possibly at the expense of others, leading to potential conflict and possibly requiring strategic behaviour."}
+{"id": "q023", "query": "Why does MYCIN not operate on patients?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_154"], "ground_truth": "MYCIN is an expert system that knows about blood diseases in humans. Unlike agents that are situated in an environment, MYCIN is not aware of the world: it only obtains information by asking the user questions, and agents act while MYCIN does not operate on patients."}
+{"id": "q024", "query": "Does an optimal agent always perform best?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_186"], "ground_truth": "No. The fact that an agent is optimal does not mean that it will be best on a particular run; it only means that on average we can expect it to do best."}
+{"id": "q025", "query": "Why can legacy components not readily be rebuilt?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_127"], "ground_truth": "In many cases the most important components of a software system will be legacy: functionally essential but technologically obsolete software components which cannot readily be rebuilt. When proposing a new software solution it is essential to work with such components."}
+{"id": "q026", "query": "What assumptions do blackboard systems make about agents accessing the board?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_73"], "ground_truth": "All of the agents can see all of the blackboards all the time, and what they see represents the current state of solution. Multiple agents (KSs/KAs) can write solutions on the blackboard at any time without getting in any other's way."}
+{"id": "q027", "query": "Describe the contract net protocol from the manager's announcement to task awarding.", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_60", "chunk_61", "chunk_62"], "ground_truth": "A manager agent announces one or several tasks; in the Recognition stage the agent realises it cannot (or prefers not to) achieve the goal in isolation; agents place bids, and the task is assigned by the manager according to an evaluation function applied to the bids, e.g. choosing the cheapest agent."}
+{"id": "q028", "query": "How do blackboard systems control which agent writes next?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_74", "chunk_75"], "ground_truth": "Blackboard systems must have some control mechanism. Effective control takes into account goal-directed factors (based on what an agent wants) and data-directed factors (based on what an agent is best able to do). Blackboard control is difficult because of complex interrelationships among agents."}
+{"id": "q029", "query": "How did agent architectures evolve from symbolic reasoning to hybrid systems?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_200"], "ground_truth": "Symbolic Reasoning Agents (1956-present) decide by manipulating symbols and using explicit logical reasoning. Problems with symbolic reasoning led to a reaction against it, giving rise to Reactive Agents (1985-present), and Hybrid architectures (1990-present) attempt to combine the best of reasoning and reactive architectures."}
+{"id": "q030", "query": "Compare auctions with negotiation for reaching agreements.", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_350"], "ground_truth": "Auctions are only concerned with the allocation of goods; richer techniques for reaching agreements are required. Negotiation is the process of reaching agreements on matters of common interest and usually proceeds in a series of rounds, with every agent making a proposal at every round."}
+{"id": "q031", "query": "What is blind commitment in commitment strategies?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_260"], "ground_truth": "Blind commitment makes the control loop more reactive and able to change intention when the world changes, but the agent is still overcommitted to its intentions (ends) and never stops to consider whether or not its intentions are appropriate."}
+{"id": "q032", "query": "What are the two key ideas behind Brooks' behavioural languages?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_275"], "ground_truth": "Brooks' research is based on two key ideas: 1. Situatedness and embodiment - real intelligence is situated in the world, not in disembodied systems such as theorem provers; 2. Intelligence and emergence - intelligent behaviour arises from an agent's interaction with its environment."}
+{"id": "q033", "query": "What is deductive argumentation?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["chunk_375"], "ground_truth": "In deductive argumentation, arguments are modelled using logical formulae. Argumentation models defeasible reasoning: conclusions can be rebutted, and premises (and warrants) can be challenged."}
