+Why is a reactive system considered easy to build for fixed environments?,"['REACTIVITY •Software is hard to build for dynamic domains: program must consider possibility of failure •A reactive system is one that maintains an ongoing interaction with its environment and responds to changes that occur in it (in time for the response to be useful). 9', 'REACTIVITY •If a program’s environment is guaranteed to be fixed, the program need never worry about its own success or failure •Program just executes blindly. •Example of fixed environment: compiler. •The real world is not like that: most environments are dynamic and information is incomplete . 8', 'LIMITATIONS OF REACTIVE SYSTEMS •As actions are based on local information, such agents inherently take a “short -term” view •Emergent behaviour is very hard to engineer or validate; typically a trial and error approach is ultimately adopted •Whilst agents with few layers are straightforward to build, models using many layers are inherently complex and difficult to understand \uf0df Design approach does not scale -up for large systems 11', 'HYBRID ARCHITECTURES •Idea: Neither completely deliberative nor completely reactive architectures are suitable → combine both perspectives in one hybrid architecture •Build an agent out of two (or more) subsystems: •a deliberative one: responsible for abstract planning and decision -making using symbolic representations •a reactive one: capable to respond to world changes without any complex reasoning and decision -making 3', 'FULLY OBSERVABLE VS PARTIALLY OBSERVABLE •An accessible or fully observable environment is one in which the agent can obtain complete, accurate, up -to-date information about the environment’s state •Most moderately complex environments (including, for example, the everyday physical world and the Internet) are inaccessible, or partially observable •The more accessible an environment is, the simpler it is to build agents to operate in it 22']","According to the context, a reactive system is considered easy to build for fixed environments because, in such cases, the program does not need to worry about its own success or failure. The environment is guaranteed to be fixed, and the program can execute blindly without considering the possibility of failure. This is in contrast to dynamic environments where the program must respond to changes in the environment and consider the possibility of failure.","If a program’s environment is guaranteed to be fixed, the program need never worry about its own success or failure. The real world is not like that: most environments are dynamic and information is incomplete.",,0.9974322812772843,0.99999999995,1.0
+What does proactiveness involve for agents?,"['PROACTIVENESS •Reacting to an environment is easy •e.g., stimulus → response rules •But we generally want agents to do things for us •Hence goal directed behaviour •Pro-activeness: generating and attempting to achieve goals; not driven solely by events; taking the initiative and recognising opportunities 10', 'AGENT ARCHITECTURES Three broad categories: 4 Deliberative Architectures focus on planning and symbolic reasoningReactive Architectures focus on reactivity based on behavioural rulesHybrid Architectures attempt to balance proactiveness with reactivity', 'RECOGNITION •In this stage, an agent recognises it has a problem it needs help with •Agent has a goal, and either •realises it cannot achieve the goal in isolation - i.e. it does not have capability •realises it would prefer not to achieve the goal in isolation (typically because of solution quality, deadline etc.) •As a result, it needs to involve other agents 6', 'WHAT IS AN AGENT? 3', 'MULTI AGENT PLANNING •Another approach to coordinate is to explicitly plan what all the agents do •Could involve: •Centralised planning for distributed plans: One agent comes up with a plan for everybody •Distributed planning: A group of agents come up with a centralised plan for another group of agents. •Distributed planning for distributed plans : Agents build up plans for themselves, but take into account the actions of others 13']","Based on the context, proactiveness involves generating and attempting to achieve goals, rather than simply reacting to events. It also involves taking the initiative and recognizing opportunities, rather than being driven solely by external stimuli.",Pro-activeness: generating and attempting to achieve goals; not driven solely by events; taking the initiative and recognising opportunities.,1.0,0.8000738078061209,0.9999999999,1.0
+"What is the difference between objects and agents, and how do agents differ from objects?","['AGENTS, OBJECTS AND AI SYSTEMS 16', 'AGENTS AND OBJECTS Are agents just objects by another name? •Object: •encapsulates some state; •communicates via message passing; •has methods, corresponding to operations that •may be performed on this state. 17', 'AGENTS AS INTENTIONAL SYSTEMS 3.Post-Declarative Systems • In procedural programming , we say exactly what a system should do; • In declarative programming , we state something that we want to achieve , give the system general info about the relationships between objects, and let a built -in control mechanism (e.g., goal -directed theorem proving) figure out what to do; • With agents, we give a high-level description of the delegated goal , and let the control mechanism figure out what to do,', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of today’s lecture, you should be able to: •Recognise agent behaviours •Compare agent with objects and AI systems •Justify agents as intentional system •Identify tasks for agents 2', 'AGENT -BASED DESIGN •Design aims to engineer , at least •What are the main components interacting within the system •What are the responsibilities and the capabilities of each component in the system •How the components interact to implement the system, i.e., the architecture of the system •Differently from analysis: we need to choose on which agents to use and how they interact 6']","Based on the provided context, the main differences between objects and agents are:
+
+1. **State and Methods**: Objects encapsulate some state and have methods corresponding to operations that may be performed on this state. Agents, on the other hand, also encapsulate some state but have a more complex behavior, as they are intentional systems.
+
+2. **Behavior and Purpose**: Objects are designed to perform specific tasks, whereas agents are designed to achieve specific goals. Agents are more autonomous and have a higher-level description of the delegated goal, whereas objects are more procedural and state-based.
+
+3. **Control Mechanism**: In procedural programming, the control mechanism is explicit, whereas in declarative programming, it is implicit. In the case of agents, the control mechanism is also implicit, as they are given a high-level description of the delegated goal and let a built-in control mechanism figure out what to do.
+
+4. **Autonomy and Intent**: Agents are intentional systems, meaning they have a purpose or goal, whereas objects are more state-based and do not necessarily have a purpose or goal.
+
+In summary, agents differ from objects in terms of their behavior, purpose, control mechanism, and autonomy. Agents are more complex and autonomous systems that are designed to achieve specific goals, whereas objects are more state-based and procedural systems.","Agents are autonomous: agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent. Agents are smart: agents are capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour. Agents are active: agents are not passive service providers; a multi-agent system is inherently multi-threaded, in that each agent is assumed to have at least one thread of active control. Objects do it because they have to! Objects do it for free! Agents do it because they want to! Agents do it for personal gain!",,0.9621230605044987,0.0,0.0
+What was the classical approach to building agents known as?,"['SOME VIEWS OF THE FIELD •We want to build systems of interacting, autonomous agents, but we don’t yet know what these systems should look like •You can take a “neat” or “scruffy” approach to the problem, seeing it as a problem of theory or a problem of engineering 37Primarily a search for appropriate theoretical foundations', 'SYMBOLIC REASONING AGENTS •The classical approach to building agents is to view them as a particular type of knowledge -based system and bring all the associated methodologies of such systems to bear. •This paradigm is known as symbolic AI •A deliberative agent or agent architecture is defined to be one that: •contains an explicitly represented, symbolic model of the world; •makes decisions (for example about what actions to perform) via symbolic reasoning. 9', 'AGENT ARCHITECTURES 4', 'SUMMARY •Defined the main agent architectures and symbolic reasoning approach •Discussed how logic enables deductive reasoning in agents •Introduced agent -oriented programming (Agent0, PLACA, Concurrent METATEM ) •Connected classical reasoning agents to modern Agentic AI systems 35', 'AGENTS IN VARIOUS FIELDS 33']",The classical approach to building agents was known as symbolic AI.,The classical approach to building agents was known as symbolic AI.,1.0,0.9999999999999997,0.499999999975,1.0
+"According to the lecture, what is the underlying problem with knowledge representation and reasoning?","['TWO ISSUES 10The Transduction Problem The transduction problem is that of translating the real world into an accurate, adequate symbolic description, in time for that description to be useful. This has led onto research into vision, speech understanding, learning… The Representation / Reasoning Problem How to symbolically represent information about complex real -world entities and processes, and how to get agents to reason with this information in time for the results to be useful.', 'THE REPRESENTATION / REASONING PROBLEM •The underlying problem with knowledge representation / reasoning lies with the complexity of symbol manipulation algorithms. •In general many (most) search -based symbol manipulation algorithms of interest are highly intractable . •Hard to find compact representations . •Because of these problems, some researchers have looked to alternative techniques for building agents; we look at these later. 11', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: •Explain how means -ends reasoning fit into the basic agent control loop •Representation •Relate blocksworld to the planning problem •Practical reasoning agent implementation •Deliberation 2', 'SYMBOLIC AI - CRITICS •Recall symbolic AI in Deductive Reasoning Agent lecture. •Is inference on symbols representing the world sufficient to solve real-world problems? •Problems with “symbolic AI”: •Computational complexity of reasoning in real -world applications •The transduction/knowledge acquisition bottleneck •Logic -based approaches largely focus on theoretical reasoning •Detached from interaction with physical world 3', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: •Describe practical reasoning •Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2']","According to the context, the underlying problem with knowledge representation and reasoning is the complexity of symbol manipulation algorithms, which makes many search-based symbol manipulation algorithms highly intractable and difficult to find compact representations.",The underlying problem with knowledge representation / reasoning lies with the complexity of symbol manipulation algorithms.,1.0,0.8628759577034483,0.499999999975,1.0
+What is the key idea of agent-oriented programming as introduced by Yoav Shoham?,"['AGENT -ORIENTED PROGRAMMING •Yoav Shoham introduced “agent -oriented programming” in 1990: “... new programming paradigm, based on a societal view of computation ...” •The key idea: •Directly programming agents in terms of intentional notions •like belief, desire, and intention •Adopts the same abstraction as humans •Resulted in the Agent0 programming language 21', 'AGENT -ORIENTED PROGRAMMING 20', 'SUMMARY •Defined the main agent architectures and symbolic reasoning approach •Discussed how logic enables deductive reasoning in agents •Introduced agent -oriented programming (Agent0, PLACA, Concurrent METATEM ) •Connected classical reasoning agents to modern Agentic AI systems 35', 'SUMMARY •In this lecture, we explored why traditional object -oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi -agent systems. •We introduced agent -oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. •We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent -based analysis and design. •We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of today’s lecture, you should be able to: •Describe agent architectures •Explain the notion of symbolic reasoning agents •Show how deductive reasoning can be achieved using logic •Differentiate types of Agent Oriented Programming 3']","The key idea of agent-oriented programming, as introduced by Yoav Shoham, is to directly program agents in terms of intentional notions such as belief, desire, and intention, adopting the same abstraction as humans.","The key idea of agent-oriented programming as introduced by Yoav Shoham is to directly program agents in terms of intentional notions like belief, desire, and intention, and to adopt the same abstraction as humans.",1.0,0.9999999999999997,0.9999999999,1.0
+What is the key component of an agent in AGENT0 that determines how the agent acts?,"['AGENT0 •The key component, which determines how the agent acts, is the commitment rule set. •Each commitment rule contains •a message condition ; •a mental condition ; and •an action 23', 'AGENT ARCHITECTURES “… [A] particular methodology for building [agents]. It specifies how … the agent can be decomposed into the construction of a set of component modules and how these modules should be made to interact . The total set of modules and their interactions has to provide an answer to the question of how the sensor data and the current internal state of the agent determine the actions … and future internal state of the agent.', 'AGENT0 •AGENT0 is implemented as an extension to LISP. •Each agent in AGENT0 has 4 components: •a set of capabilities (things the agent can do); •a set of initial beliefs ; •a set of initial commitments (things the agent will do); and •a set of commitment rules . 22', 'SUMMARY •This lecture has looked in detail at what constitutes an intelligent agent •We looked at the properties of an intelligent agent and the properties of the environments in which it may operate •We introduced the intentional stance and discussed its use •Finally, we discussed what kinds of task an agent might need to carry out. 50', 'SUMMARY •Overall, of the three lectures: •Key assumption: benevolence •Agents are working together, not in competition •In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14']","The key component of an agent in AGENT0 that determines how the agent acts is the ""commitment rule set"".","The key component, which determines how the agent acts, is the commitment rule set.",1.0,0.9999999999999992,0.8333333332916666,1.0
