+"What is the role of intentions in practical reasoning, as explained in the lecture?","['LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: •Describe practical reasoning •Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2', '•Human practical reasoning consists of two activities: •The interplay between beliefs, desires and intentions defines how the model worksCOMPONENTS OF PRACTICAL REASONING 4DELIBERATION •deciding what situation, we want to achieve •the outputs of deliberation are intentionsMEANS -ENDS REASONING •deciding how to achieve these states of affairs •the outputs of means -ends reasoning are plans', 'SUMMARY •We have define practical reasoning. •We acknowledge how intentions are key part of practical reasoning. •How intention relates to practical reasoning •Intentions are stronger than desire •Next → Means -ends Reasoning/Planning 9', 'INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 3.Agents track the success of their intentions and are inclined to try again if their attempts fail. 4.Agents believe their intentions are possible. 6', 'INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 1.Intentions pose problems for agents, who need to determine ways of achieving them. 2.Intentions provide a “filter” for adopting other intentions, which must not conflict. 5']","According to the lecture, intentions play a crucial role in practical reasoning. The key points about intentions in practical reasoning are:
+
+1. Intentions pose problems for agents, who need to determine ways of achieving them.
+2. Intentions provide a ""filter"" for adopting other intentions, which must not conflict.
+3. Agents track the success of their intentions and are inclined to try again if their attempts fail.
+4. Agents believe their intentions are possible.
+
+In summary, intentions are a key component of practical reasoning, as they define what the agent wants to achieve (means-ends reasoning) and guide the agent's decision-making process.","Intention poses problems for agents, who need to determine ways of achieving them. Intention provides a 'filter' for adopting other intentions which must not conflict.",0.9166666666666666,0.9505888519373805,0.19999999998,1.0
+What does the 'side effect or package deal problem' refer to in the context of practical reasoning?,"['INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 7.Agents need not intend all the expected side effects of their intentions. I may believe that going to the dentist involves pain, and I may also intend to go to the dentist — but this does not imply that I intend to suffer pain! 8 This is known as the side effect or package deal problem.', 'WHAT IS PRACTICAL REASONING? •Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do •Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes (Bratman) •Distinguish practical reasoning from theoretical reasoning – directed towards belief 3', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: •Describe practical reasoning •Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2', '•Human practical reasoning consists of two activities: •The interplay between beliefs, desires and intentions defines how the model worksCOMPONENTS OF PRACTICAL REASONING 4DELIBERATION •deciding what situation, we want to achieve •the outputs of deliberation are intentionsMEANS -ENDS REASONING •deciding how to achieve these states of affairs •the outputs of means -ends reasoning are plans', 'SUMMARY •We have define practical reasoning. •We acknowledge how intentions are key part of practical reasoning. •How intention relates to practical reasoning •Intentions are stronger than desire •Next → Means -ends Reasoning/Planning 9']","In the context of practical reasoning, the 'side effect or package deal problem' refers to the situation where an agent may intend to do something, but not necessarily intend all the expected side effects or consequences of that action. This means that an agent can have a specific intention without necessarily intending every single outcome or side effect that may result from that action.","The side effect or package deal problem refers to the fact that I may believe that going to the dentist involves pain, and I may also intend to go to the dentist — but this does not imply that I intend to suffer pain!",0.6,0.9919135453452902,0.9999999999,1.0
+What is the goal in the blocks world problem?,"['BLOCKSWORLD •Let’s look at the planning problem with reference to the blocks world. •The blocks world contains: •a robot arm, •3 blocks (A, B and C) of equal size •a table -top •The aim is to generate a plan for the robot arm to build towers out of blocks 5', 'BLOCKSWORLD •The environment is represented by an ontology •The closed world assumption is used - Anything not stated is assumed to be false. •A goal is represented as a set of formulae. 6', 'TWO ISSUES 10The Transduction Problem The transduction problem is that of translating the real world into an accurate, adequate symbolic description, in time for that description to be useful. This has led onto research into vision, speech understanding, learning… The Representation / Reasoning Problem How to symbolically represent information about complex real -world entities and processes, and how to get agents to reason with this information in time for the results to be useful.', 'RECOGNITION •In this stage, an agent recognises it has a problem it needs help with •Agent has a goal, and either •realises it cannot achieve the goal in isolation - i.e. it does not have capability •realises it would prefer not to achieve the goal in isolation (typically because of solution quality, deadline etc.) •As a result, it needs to involve other agents 6', 'RESULTS SHARING IN BLACKBOARD SYSTEMS •The first scheme for cooperative problem solving → the blackboard system •Metaphor: A collection of intelligent agents gather around a blackboard, look at pieces of information written on it, think about them, and add their conclusions •Assumptions: •All of the agents can see all of the blackboards (BB) all the time, and what they see represents the current state of solution •Multiple agents (KSs/KAs) can write solution on the blackboard at anytime without']",The goal in the blocks world problem is to generate a plan for the robot arm to build towers out of blocks.,The aim is to generate a plan for the robot arm to build towers out of blocks.,1.0,0.9999999999999992,0.9999999999,1.0
+What is the role of the Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS) in planning?,"['REPRESENTATION } 4•goal to be achieved •state of environment •actions available to agent •plan itselfRepresented by logic The Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS): an automated planner used by Shakey Use first -order logic and theorem proving to plan strategies from start to goal', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: •Explain how means -ends reasoning fit into the basic agent control loop •Representation •Relate blocksworld to the planning problem •Practical reasoning agent implementation •Deliberation 2', 'MULTI AGENT PLANNING •Another approach to coordinate is to explicitly plan what all the agents do •Could involve: •Centralised planning for distributed plans: One agent comes up with a plan for everybody •Distributed planning: A group of agents come up with a centralised plan for another group of agents. •Distributed planning for distributed plans : Agents build up plans for themselves, but take into account the actions of others 13', 'MEANS -ENDS REASONING / P LANNING •Planning: The design of a course of action that will achieve some desired goal •Basically, give a planning system representation of: •goal/intention to achieve; •actions it can perform; •the environment; •and generate a plan to achieve the goal 3 Let the system figure out how to achieve the goal on its own!', 'BLOCKSWORLD •Let’s look at the planning problem with reference to the blocks world. •The blocks world contains: •a robot arm, •3 blocks (A, B and C) of equal size •a table -top •The aim is to generate a plan for the robot arm to build towers out of blocks 5']","Based on the context, the Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS) is an automated planner used by Shakey. It uses first-order logic and theorem proving to plan strategies from start to goal, representing planning by logic.",Use first-order logic and theorem proving to plan strategies from start to goal.,1.0,0.8995504661284445,0.9999999999,1.0
+How does an agent select an intention to achieve after observing the world?,"['INTENTION RECONSIDERATION •An agent that does not stop to reconsider its intentions sufficiently often will continue to attempt to achieve its intentions even after it is clear that they cannot be achieved, or that there is no longer any reason for achieving them. •An agent that constantly reconsiders its attentions may spend insufficient time actually working to achieve them, and hence runs the risk of never actually achieving them. 9', 'DELIBERATION •How does an agent deliberate? •begin by trying to understand what the options available to you are •choose between them and commit to some •Chosen options are then intentions •The deliberate function can be decomposed into two distinct functional components: •option generation •filtering 12', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: •Consider commitment and intention in an agent •Degrees of commitment •Intention reconsideration 2', 'OPTIMAL INTENTION RECONSIDERATION •Kinny and Georgeff’s experimentally investigated effectiveness of intention reconsideration strategies •Two different types of reconsideration strategy were used: •bold agents : never pause to reconsider intentions, and •cautious agents : stop to reconsider after every action. •Dynamism in the environment is represented by the rate of world change, γ. •Experiments were carried out using Tileword . 11', 'RECOGNITION •In this stage, an agent recognises it has a problem it needs help with •Agent has a goal, and either •realises it cannot achieve the goal in isolation - i.e. it does not have capability •realises it would prefer not to achieve the goal in isolation (typically because of solution quality, deadline etc.) •As a result, it needs to involve other agents 6']","Based on the context, it appears that an agent selects an intention to achieve after observing the world through a process of deliberation, which involves:
+
+1. Generating options
+2. Filtering these options
+3. Choosing between them
+4. Committing to a chosen option, which becomes an intention.
+
+This process is described in [S2] as the ""deliberation"" function, which can be decomposed into two distinct functional components: option generation and filtering.",The agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world.,0.75,0.5483576254626356,0.0,0.0
+What does the filter function in option generation and filtering do?,"['OPTION GENERATION AND FILTERING OPTION GENERATION FILTERING The agent generates a set of possible alternativesThe agent chooses between competing alternatives, and commits to achieving them Represent option generation via a function, options(), which takes the agent’s current beliefs and current intentions, and from them determines a set of optionsIn order to select between competing options, an agent uses a filter() function Desires Intentions options : 𝒫(Bel) ×𝒫(Int) ➝𝒫(Des) filter : 𝒫(Bel) ×', 'DELIBERATION •How does an agent deliberate? •begin by trying to understand what the options available to you are •choose between them and commit to some •Chosen options are then intentions •The deliberate function can be decomposed into two distinct functional components: •option generation •filtering 12', 'agent uses a filter() function Desires Intentions options : 𝒫(Bel) ×𝒫(Int) ➝𝒫(Des) filter : 𝒫(Bel) × 𝒫(Des) × 𝒫(Int) ➝ 𝒫(Int) 13', 'SUMMARY •We have looked at how goal, state, action and plan can be represented using logic. •We have used the blocksworld problem as an example •We have looked at reasoning agent implementation •We discussed deliberation and decomposed it to option generation and filtering •Next → Commitment 14', 'AGENTS WITH STATE - PERCEPT 40The see function is the agent’s ability to observe its environment, whereas the action function represents the agent’s decision -making process. Output of the see function is a percept: ...which maps environment states to percepts.']","According to the context, the filter function in option generation and filtering takes the agent's current beliefs and desires as input, and returns a set of options that align with the agent's desires. In other words, it filters out options that do not match the agent's desires.
+
+More specifically, the filter function is defined as:
+
+filter : 𝒫(Bel) × 𝒫(Des) × 𝒫(Int) ➝ 𝒫(Int)
+
+This means that it takes three inputs:
+
+1. Beliefs (𝒫(Bel))
+2. Desires (𝒫(Des))
+3. Intentions (𝒫(Int))
+
+And returns a set of intentions (𝒫(Int)).
+
+In essence, the filter function helps the agent to narrow down its options to those that are consistent with its desires and intentions.","In order to select between competing options, an agent uses a filter() function.",,0.9471756898972686,0.9999999999666667,1.0
+What is the representation of the goal in the STRIPS planning system?,"['MEANS -ENDS REASONING / P LANNING •Planning: The design of a course of action that will achieve some desired goal •Basically, give a planning system representation of: •goal/intention to achieve; •actions it can perform; •the environment; •and generate a plan to achieve the goal 3 Let the system figure out how to achieve the goal on its own!', 'REPRESENTATION } 4•goal to be achieved •state of environment •actions available to agent •plan itselfRepresented by logic The Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS): an automated planner used by Shakey Use first -order logic and theorem proving to plan strategies from start to goal', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: •Explain how means -ends reasoning fit into the basic agent control loop •Representation •Relate blocksworld to the planning problem •Practical reasoning agent implementation •Deliberation 2', 'MULTI AGENT PLANNING •Another approach to coordinate is to explicitly plan what all the agents do •Could involve: •Centralised planning for distributed plans: One agent comes up with a plan for everybody •Distributed planning: A group of agents come up with a centralised plan for another group of agents. •Distributed planning for distributed plans : Agents build up plans for themselves, but take into account the actions of others 13', 'high-level description of the delegated goal , and let the control mechanism figure out what to do, knowing that it will act in accordance with some built-in theory of rational agency 36']","According to the context, the STRIPS planning system uses first-order logic and theorem proving to represent the goal.","Represented by logic, the STRIPS planning system uses first-order logic and theorem proving to plan strategies from start to goal.",0.5,0.952926770222187,0.49999999995,1.0
