+"What does the evolution towards agentic AI bring us, according to the lecture?","['EVOLUTION TOWARD AGENTIC AI Era Agent Type Key Feature Example 1990s Reactive Respond to stimuli Subsumption robot 2000s Deliberative Plan before acting BDI model (Poole & Mackworth Ch. 4) 2010s Cognitive Learn & reason IBM Watson 2020s Agentic AI Use LLMs to understand, plan & act ChatGPT Agents, AutoGPT 22', 'AGENTIC AI • Agentic AI refers to AI systems that can act independently, make decisions, and perform tasks to achieve specific goals. • These systems combine reasoning, memory, and generative abilities to plan and adapt their actions. • Unlike traditional AI, they don’t just respond — they take initiative and collaborate with humans or other agents. 26', 'INTENTIONAL STANCE IN TODAY’S AI • LLMs and agentic AI often seem to make choices or have goals • When we say “ChatGPT understands” or “the agent decides,” we’re using the intentional stance • These systems don’t truly think, but this view helps us explain and predict their behaviour • Talking about AI “as if it thinks” makes human–AI interaction easier to understand. 37', 'SUMMARY • This lecture has looked in detail at what constitutes an intelligent agent • We looked at the properties of an intelligent agent and the properties of the environments in which it may operate • We introduced the intentional stance and discussed its use • Finally, we discussed what kinds of task an agent might need to carry out. 50', 'SUMMARY • Overall, of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14']","Based on the context, the evolution towards agentic AI brings us the ability for AI systems to act independently, make decisions, and perform tasks to achieve specific goals. These systems combine reasoning, memory, and generative abilities to plan and adapt their actions, taking initiative and collaborating with humans or other agents.","The ability of computer systems to act independently, the ability of computer systems to act in a way that represents our best interests while interacting with other humans or systems",1.0,0.8262115945579822,0.49999999995,0.5
+"What are some examples of generative AI models that modern agents may use, according to the lecture?",[],"I don't have any information about a specific lecture. However, I can provide some examples of generative AI models that modern agents may use:
+
+1. **Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)**: A type of deep learning model that uses a competitive process to generate new data that resembles existing data.
+2. **Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)**: A type of deep learning model that learns to compress and reconstruct data, and can be used for generative purposes.
+3. **Transformers**: A type of neural network architecture that is particularly well-suited for sequence-to-sequence tasks, such as text generation.
+4. **Diffusion Models**: A type of generative model that uses a process called diffusion to generate new data.
+5. **Neural Style Transfer**: A type of generative model that can transfer the style of one image to another.
+6. **Image Synthesis**: Models like CycleGAN, Pix2Pix, and DCGAN, which can generate new images from existing images.
+7. **Text-to-Text Transformers**: Models like BART and T5, which can generate new text based on existing text.
+
+These are just a few examples of the many generative AI models that modern agents may use. The specific models used would depend on the application and the goals of the agent.","Modern agents may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming",0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0
+What is the main point about agents being autonomous?,"['WHAT IS AN AGENT? • The main point about agents is they are autonomous: capable independent action 4 An agent is a computer system that is situated in some environment, and that is capable of autonomous action in that environment in order to meet its delegated objectives • It is all about decisions: • An agent must choose what action to perform • An agent must decide when to perform an action', 'AGENTS: A FIRST DEFINITION • An agent is a computer system that is capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of its user or owner • I.e., figuring out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives, rather than constantly being told • Modern agents may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming 24', '18 Agents are autonomous: • agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent Agents are smart: • capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour Agents are active: • not passive service providers;', 'AUTONOMOUS • By autonomous mobile, we mean agents that are able to decide for themselves where to go, when, and what to do when they get there (subject to certain resource constraints) • Such agents are generally programmed in a special language that provides a go instruction • best known example is TELESCRIPT 12', 'SOME VIEWS OF THE FIELD • We want to build systems of interacting, autonomous agents, but we don’t yet know what these systems should look like • You can take a “neat” or “scruffy” approach to the problem, seeing it as a problem of theory or a problem of engineering 37 Primarily a search for appropriate theoretical foundations']","The main point about agents being autonomous is that they are capable of independent action in their environment, making decisions on what action to perform and when to perform it, without constant external instruction.",The main point about agents is they are autonomous: capable of independent action.,1.0,0.9999999999999996,0.99999999998,1.0
+What types of behaviour does an intelligent agent typically exhibit?,"['INTELLIGENT AGENT It is typically thought as exhibiting 3 types of behaviour: • Reactive • Pro-active • Social Ability 7 An intelligent agent is a computer system capable of flexible autonomous action in some environment', 'SUMMARY • This lecture has looked in detail at what constitutes an intelligent agent • We looked at the properties of an intelligent agent and the properties of the environments in which it may operate • We introduced the intentional stance and discussed its use • Finally, we discussed what kinds of task an agent might need to carry out. 50', '18 Agents are autonomous: • agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent Agents are smart: • capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour Agents are active: • not passive service providers;', 'INTELLIGENT AGENTS AND AI • When building an agent, we simply want a system that can choose the right action to perform, typically in a limited domain • We do not have to solve all the problems of AI to build a useful agent → a little intelligence goes a long way! 20 “We made our agents dumber and dumber and dumber . . . until finally they made money.” - Oren Etzioni regarding NETBOT, Inc.', 'PROPERTIES OF ENVIRONMENTS • Since agents are in close contact with their environment, the properties of the environment affect agents and those who build agents • Common to categorise environments along some different dimensions 21 Fully observable vs partially observable Deterministic vs non-deterministic Static vs dynamic Discrete vs continuous Episodic vs non-episodic Real Time']","According to the context, an intelligent agent typically exhibits 3 types of behavior:
