+What is the key idea of agent-oriented programming as introduced by Yoav Shoham?,"['AGENT-ORIENTED PROGRAMMING • Yoav Shoham introduced “agent-oriented programming” in 1990: “... new programming paradigm, based on a societal view of computation ...” • The key idea: • Directly programming agents in terms of intentional notions • like belief, desire, and intention • Adopts the same abstraction as humans • Resulted in the Agent0 programming language 21', 'AGENT-ORIENTED PROGRAMMING 20', 'SUMMARY • Defined the main agent architectures and symbolic reasoning approach • Discussed how logic enables deductive reasoning in agents • Introduced agent-oriented programming (Agent0, PLACA, Concurrent METATEM) • Connected classical reasoning agents to modern Agentic AI systems 35', 'SUMMARY • In this lecture, we explored why traditional object-oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi-agent systems. • We introduced agent-oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. • We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent-based analysis and design. • We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23', 'AGENT COMMUNICATION LANGUAGES 10']","The key idea of agent-oriented programming, as introduced by Yoav Shoham, is to directly program agents in terms of intentional notions such as belief, desire, and intention, adopting the same abstraction as humans.","The key idea of agent-oriented programming as introduced by Yoav Shoham is to directly program agents in terms of intentional notions like belief, desire, and intention, and to adopt the same abstraction as humans.",1.0,0.9999999999999997,0.9999999999,1.0
+What is the key component of an agent in AGENT0 that determines how the agent acts?,"['AGENT0 • The key component, which determines how the agent acts, is the commitment rule set. • Each commitment rule contains • a message condition; • a mental condition; and • an action 23', 'AGENT ARCHITECTURES “… [A] particular methodology for building [agents]. It specifies how … the agent can be decomposed into the construction of a set of component modules and how these modules should be made to interact. The total set of modules and their interactions has to provide an answer to the question of how the sensor data and the current internal state of the agent determine the actions … and future internal state of the agent.', 'AGENT0 • AGENT0 is implemented as an extension to LISP. • Each agent in AGENT0 has 4 components: • a set of capabilities (things the agent can do); • a set of initial beliefs; • a set of initial commitments (things the agent will do); and • a set of commitment rules. 22', 'SUMMARY • This lecture has looked in detail at what constitutes an intelligent agent • We looked at the properties of an intelligent agent and the properties of the environments in which it may operate • We introduced the intentional stance and discussed its use • Finally, we discussed what kinds of task an agent might need to carry out. 50', 'SUMMARY • Overall, of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14']","The key component of an agent in AGENT0 that determines how the agent acts is the ""commitment rule set"".","The key component, which determines how the agent acts, is the commitment rule set.",1.0,0.9999999999999992,0.8333333332916666,1.0
+"In AGENT0, what is the difference between a private action and a communicative action?",['AGENT0 DECISION CYCLE • Actions may be: • Private: An externally executed computation • Communicative: Sending messages • Messages are constrained to be one of three types 1. Requests: To commit to action 2. Unrequests: To refrain from action 3. Informs: Which pass on information 25'],"In AGENT0, the difference between a private action and a communicative action is:
