+What is the main point about agents being autonomous?,"['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAGENTS: A FIRST DEFINITION • An agent is a computer system that is capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of its user or owner • I.e., figuring out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives, rather than constantly being told • Modern agents may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming 24', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSOME VIEWS OF THE FIELD • We want to build systems of interacting, autonomous agents, but we don’t yet know what these systems should look like • You can take a “neat” or “scruffy” approach to the problem, seeing it as a problem of theory or a problem of engineering 37 Primarily a search for appropriate theoretical foundations', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAUTONOMOUS • By autonomous mobile, we mean agents that are able to decide for themselves where to go, when, and what to do when they get there (subject to certain resource constraints) • Such agents are generally programmed in a special language that provides a go instruction • best known example is TELESCRIPT 12', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nTHE MICRO AND MACRO PROBLEMS 32 Agent Design Society Design How do we build agents that are capable of independent, autonomous action in order to successfully carry out the tasks that we delegate to them? How can we create agents that cooperate, coordinate, and negotiate with one another to complete the tasks we assign, even when they have different goals or interests?', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • Overall, of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14']","According to the context, the main point about agents being autonomous is that they are capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of their user or owner, meaning they can figure out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives without constant instruction.",The main point about agents is they are autonomous: capable of independent action.,1.0,0.986122708960071,0.999999999975,1.0
+What types of behaviour does an intelligent agent typically exhibit?,"['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSOCIAL NORMS • Societies are often regulated by (often unwritten) rules of behaviour • Example: • A group of people is waiting at the bus stop. The bus arrives. Who gets on the bus first? • There’s only one seat on the bus and there’s a young man and an elderly man. Who gets the seat? • In an agent system, we can design the norms and program agents to follow them, or let norms evolve 8', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nEMERGENCE - STRATEGIES Simple majority: • Agents pick the shirt they have seen the most Simple majority with types: • Agents come in two types. When they meet an agent of the same type, agents pass their memories. Otherwise, they act as simple majority. Highest cumulative reward: • Agents can “see” how often other agents (some subset of all the agents) have matched their pair. They pick the shirt with the largest number of matches. 10', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nRECOGNITION • In this stage, an agent recognises it has a problem it needs help with • Agent has a goal, and either • realises it cannot achieve the goal in isolation - i.e. it does not have capability • realises it would prefer not to achieve the goal in isolation (typically because of solution quality, deadline etc.) • As a result, it needs to involve other agents 6', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAGENTS: A FIRST DEFINITION • An agent is a computer system that is capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of its user or owner • I.e., figuring out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives, rather than constantly being told • Modern agents may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming 24', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAGENT MOTIVATIONS 5 Self Interested Agents • If agents represent the interests of the general, then we cannot make the benevolence assumption • Agents will be assumed to act to further their own interests, possibly at the expense of others • Potential for conflict • May complicate the design task enormously • Strategic behaviour may be required']","Based on the provided context, an intelligent agent typically exhibits the following types of behaviour:
+
+1. Autonomous action: Agents can take independent action on behalf of their user or owner, without constantly being told what to do.
+2. Self-interested behaviour: Agents are assumed to act in their own interests, possibly at the expense of others, which can lead to potential conflicts and strategic behaviour.
+3. Problem-solving: Agents can figure out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives, and may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming.
+4. Recognition of the need for help: Agents can recognise that they have a problem they need help with, and may seek assistance from other agents.
+5. Coordination and cooperation: Agents can design norms and program themselves to follow these norms, or let norms evolve, and may use strategies such as simple majority or highest cumulative reward to coordinate their behaviour.
+
+These types of behaviour are typical of intelligent agents, as described in the context of the course BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT.","An intelligent agent is a computer system capable of flexible autonomous action in some environment. It exhibits 3 types of behaviour: reactive, pro-active, and social ability.",1.0,0.9114429657343601,0.0,0.5
+Why is a reactive system considered easy to build for fixed environments?,"['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • In this lecture, we explored why traditional object-oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi-agent systems. • We introduced agent-oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. • We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent-based analysis and design. • We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSOME VIEWS OF THE FIELD • We want to build systems of interacting, autonomous agents, but we don’t yet know what these systems should look like • You can take a “neat” or “scruffy” approach to the problem, seeing it as a problem of theory or a problem of engineering 37 Primarily a search for appropriate theoretical foundations', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nTELESCRIPT • Rich environment for building language-based applications, particularly GUI-based ones • Not intended as agent programming environments • Source code is free, stable, well-designed, and easily modified 22 • Rich set of primitives for building distributed applications, with a fairly powerful notion of agency • Agents are ultimately interpreted programs • Actual impact more likely to be in the realm of ideas, rather than instantiation TCL/TKVS', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nWANT GENERIC SOLUTIONS • Devising a generic architecture/testbed, when really there is need a tailor-made system • Re-use is difficult to attain unless development is undertaken for a close-knit range of problems with similar characteristics • General solutions are more difficult and more costly to develop 7 Macro (Society) LevelPolitical Management Conceptual Analysis & Design Micro (Agent) Level', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nTHE MICRO AND MACRO PROBLEMS 32 Agent Design Society Design How do we build agents that are capable of independent, autonomous action in order to successfully carry out the tasks that we delegate to them? How can we create agents that cooperate, coordinate, and negotiate with one another to complete the tasks we assign, even when they have different goals or interests?']","According to the context, a reactive system is considered easy to build for fixed environments because it can simply respond to the current state of the environment without needing to consider the future or complex interactions with other agents. This is in contrast to more complex systems that require agents to reason, plan, and coordinate with each other, which is a more challenging task.","If a program’s environment is guaranteed to be fixed, the program need never worry about its own success or failure. The real world is not like that: most environments are dynamic and information is incomplete.",0.1111111111111111,0.9974322812772843,0.0,0.0
+What does proactiveness involve for agents?,"['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nRECOGNITION • In this stage, an agent recognises it has a problem it needs help with • Agent has a goal, and either • realises it cannot achieve the goal in isolation - i.e. it does not have capability • realises it would prefer not to achieve the goal in isolation (typically because of solution quality, deadline etc.) • As a result, it needs to involve other agents 6', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • Overall, of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nCONTROL MECHANISM • Blackboard systems must have some control mechanism: • Effective control requires to take into account: • Goal-directed factors: based on what an agent wants • Data-directed factors: based on what an agent is best able to do • Blackboard control is difficult because it can be difficult to determine the expected value of an action by an agent as there may be complex interrelationships among the agents 7', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nWHAT IF THE INTERNET AGENTS BECOME BETTER? • Internet agents need not simply search • They can plan, arrange, buy, negotiate – carry out arrangements of all sorts that would normally be done by their human user • As more can be done electronically, software agents theoretically have more access to systems that affect the real- world 30', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nMULTI AGENT PLANNING • Another approach to coordinate is to explicitly plan what all the agents do • Could involve: • Centralised planning for distributed plans: One agent comes up with a plan for everybody • Distributed planning: A group of agents come up with a centralised plan for another group of agents. • Distributed planning for distributed plans: Agents build up plans for themselves, but take into account the actions of others 13']","Based on the context, proactiveness involves an agent anticipating potential problems and taking initiative to involve other agents for help, rather than waiting until it recognizes it has a problem and needs assistance. This is mentioned in the ""RECOGNITION"" stage, where it is stated that an agent recognizes it has a problem and needs help, and then decides to involve other agents.",Pro-activeness: generating and attempting to achieve goals; not driven solely by events; taking the initiative and recognising opportunities.,0.2,0.7942106386184525,0.0,0.0
+"What is the difference between objects and agents, and how do agents differ from objects?","['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nABSTRACTIONS IN PROGRAMMING • Most important developments in computing are based on new abstractions • Just as moving from machine code to higher-level languages brings an efficiency gain, so does moving from objects to agents • Agents, as intentional systems, that represent a further, and increasingly powerful abstraction 17 Machine code Assembly language Machine-independent language Sub-routines Procedures and functions Abstract data types Objects Agents', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nTHE MICRO AND MACRO PROBLEMS 32 Agent Design Society Design How do we build agents that are capable of independent, autonomous action in order to successfully carry out the tasks that we delegate to them? How can we create agents that cooperate, coordinate, and negotiate with one another to complete the tasks we assign, even when they have different goals or interests?', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • In this lecture, we explored why traditional object-oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi-agent systems. • We introduced agent-oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. • We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent-based analysis and design. • We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • Overall, of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nWORKING TOGETHER • Since agents are autonomous, they have to make decisions at run-time, and be capable of dynamic coordination • Overall, they will need to be able to share: • Tasks • Information • No common goals if agents designed by different individuals • Important to make a distinction between: • benevolent agents and • self-interested agents 3']","Based on the provided context, the difference between objects and agents is that objects are a lower-level abstraction in programming, whereas agents are a higher-level abstraction.
+
+Objects are represented on page 17 of the 1.pdf source, which lists them as a lower-level abstraction compared to other programming concepts such as machine code, assembly language, and machine-independent language.
+
+Agents, on the other hand, are mentioned as a further and increasingly powerful abstraction on the same page, indicating that they represent a higher level of abstraction than objects.
+
+In essence, objects are individual entities with properties and behaviors, whereas agents are intentional systems that can represent a group or a collection of objects, and are capable of independent, autonomous action. Agents are also more complex and powerful than objects, as they can interact with each other and their environment, and can have different goals and interests.","Agents are autonomous: agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent. Agents are smart: agents are capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour. Agents are active: agents are not passive service providers; a multi-agent system is inherently multi-threaded, in that each agent is assumed to have at least one thread of active control. Objects do it because they have to! Objects do it for free! Agents do it because they want to! Agents do it for personal gain!",,0.9624637804440939,0.4499999999775,0.14285714285714285
+What was the classical approach to building agents known as?,"['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSOME VIEWS OF THE FIELD • We want to build systems of interacting, autonomous agents, but we don’t yet know what these systems should look like • You can take a “neat” or “scruffy” approach to the problem, seeing it as a problem of theory or a problem of engineering 37 Primarily a search for appropriate theoretical foundations', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nMULTI AGENT PLANNING • Another approach to coordinate is to explicitly plan what all the agents do • Could involve: • Centralised planning for distributed plans: One agent comes up with a plan for everybody • Distributed planning: A group of agents come up with a centralised plan for another group of agents. • Distributed planning for distributed plans: Agents build up plans for themselves, but take into account the actions of others 13', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • In this lecture, we explored why traditional object-oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi-agent systems. • We introduced agent-oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. • We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent-based analysis and design. • We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nCOOPERATIVE DISTRIBUTED PROBLEM SOLVING (CDPS) CPDS addresses the following: 1. Problem decomposition: How can a problem be divided into smaller tasks for distribution amongst agents? 2. Sub-problem solution: • How can the overall problem-solving activities of the agents be optimised so as to produce a solution that maximises the coherence metric? • What techniques can be used to coordinate the activity of the agents, thus avoiding destructive interactions? 3. Solution synthesis:', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • Overall, of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14']","The classical approach to building agents was known as a ""neat"" or ""scruffy"" approach, which was seen as either a problem of theory or a problem of engineering, primarily focusing on finding appropriate theoretical foundations.",The classical approach to building agents was known as symbolic AI.,0.0,0.9727553028541501,0.0,0.0
+"According to the lecture, what is the underlying problem with knowledge representation and reasoning?","['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nLEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Understand the motivation behind Cooperative Distributed Problem Solving (CDPS) • Explain how CDPS can help agents to solve problems that are beyond their individual capabilities • Compare task sharing and result sharing • The contract net • Blackboard systems • Subscribe/notify pattern 2', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nJOINT INTENTIONS • The termination condition is achieved when an agent realizes that the goal is satisfied, impossible, the motivation is no longer present and so on. • But it doesn’t drop the goal right away • Instead, it adopts a new goal — to make this new knowledge mutually believed → this ensures that the agents are coordinated • They don’t stop working towards the goal until they are all appraised of the situation • Mutual belief is achieved by communication 12', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nCOOPERATIVE DISTRIBUTED PROBLEM SOLVING (CDPS) CPDS addresses the following: 1. Problem decomposition: How can a problem be divided into smaller tasks for distribution amongst agents? 2. Sub-problem solution: • How can the overall problem-solving activities of the agents be optimised so as to produce a solution that maximises the coherence metric? • What techniques can be used to coordinate the activity of the agents, thus avoiding destructive interactions? 3. Solution synthesis:', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. 