+What is the key component of an agent in AGENT0 that determines how the agent acts?,"['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • Overall, of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAGENT-BASED ANALYSIS • Analysis aims to understand, at least • What are the main actors interacting with the system • How the system interacts with these actors • What the system is supposed to do • Then, we associate accordingly • Roles responsibilities and capabilities • Interaction patterns between agents 5', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAGENT-BASED DESIGN • Design aims to engineer, at least • What are the main components interacting within the system • What are the responsibilities and the capabilities of each component in the system • How the components interact to implement the system, i.e., the architecture of the system • Differently from analysis: we need to choose on which agents to use and how they interact 6', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nTHE MICRO AND MACRO PROBLEMS 32 Agent Design Society Design How do we build agents that are capable of independent, autonomous action in order to successfully carry out the tasks that we delegate to them? How can we create agents that cooperate, coordinate, and negotiate with one another to complete the tasks we assign, even when they have different goals or interests?', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAGENT ORIENTED METHODOLOGIES • Each methodology may introduce further abstractions - around which to model software and to organize the software process • For example: roles, organizations, responsibilities, belief, desire and intentions • Not directly translating into concrete entities of the software system → the concept of role is an aspect of an agent, not an agent 4']","Based on the context, the key component of an agent in AGENT0 that determines how the agent acts is not explicitly stated in the provided texts. However, considering the overall structure of the course materials, it can be inferred that the key component is likely related to the ""beliefs, desires, and intentions"" (BDI) model.
+
