 of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nWORKING TOGETHER • Since agents are autonomous, they have to make decisions at run-time, and be capable of dynamic coordination • Overall, they will need to be able to share: • Tasks • Information • No common goals if agents designed by different individuals • Important to make a distinction between: • benevolent agents and • self-interested agents 3']","Based on the context, a private action refers to an action that an agent takes independently, without any external influence or coordination with other agents. This type of action is autonomous and is driven by the agent's internal goals and objectives.
+
+On the other hand, a communicative action refers to an action that an agent takes in response to information or instructions received from other agents. This type of action is dependent on external input and is driven by the agent's need to coordinate or cooperate with other agents.
