00 we can get 10% of that • Managers propose agent projects without having clear idea about what “having agents” will buy them • Often, projects appear to be going well but no vision about where to go with them → No business plan', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nINCONSISTENCY • A group of agents may have inconsistencies in their: • Beliefs • Goals or intentions • Inconsistent beliefs arise because agents have different views of the world • May be due to sensor faults or noise or just because they can’t see everything • Inconsistent goals may arise because agents are built by different people with different objectives 10']","Unfortunately, there is no text provided that mentions Bratman or his suggestions about practical reasoning in the context of the given sources. The provided sources appear to be related to the topic of Intelligent Agents, but they do not mention Bratman or his ideas on practical reasoning.","Bratman suggests that practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes.",1.0,0.9031432563695417,0.0,0.0
+Explain the distinction between practical reasoning and theoretical reasoning as discussed in the lecture.,"['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nLEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Understand the need of agent-oriented methodologies • State different types of agent-oriented methodologies • Explain mobile agents by discussing the needs, issues and typology associated to it • Differentiate Telescript and TCL/TK 2', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAGENTIC AI • Agentic AI refers to AI systems that can act independently, make decisions, and perform tasks to achieve specific goals. • These systems combine reasoning, memory, and generative abilities to plan and adapt their actions. • Unlike traditional AI, they don’t just respond — they take initiative and collaborate with humans or other agents. 26', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSOME VIEWS OF THE FIELD • We want to build systems of interacting, autonomous agents, but we don’t yet know what these systems should look like • You can take a “neat” or “scruffy” approach to the problem, seeing it as a problem of theory or a problem of engineering 37 Primarily a search for appropriate theoretical foundations', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • In this lecture, we explored why traditional object-oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi-agent systems. • We introduced agent-oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. • We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent-based analysis and design. • We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nINCONSISTENCY • A group of agents may have inconsistencies in their: • Beliefs • Goals or intentions • Inconsistent beliefs arise because agents have different views of the world • May be due to sensor faults or noise or just because they can’t see everything • Inconsistent goals may arise because agents are built by different people with different objectives 10']","Unfortunately, the provided text does not explicitly discuss the distinction between practical reasoning and theoretical reasoning. However, based on the context of the lecture on Intelligent Agents, it can be inferred that the distinction might be related to the approach taken in designing and analyzing multi-agent systems.
+
+Practical reasoning might refer to the process of designing and implementing agent-based systems, focusing on the practical aspects of creating systems that can interact, adapt, and achieve specific goals. This approach would involve considering the needs, issues, and typology associated with mobile agents, as well as the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity.
