+"What is the role of intentions in practical reasoning, as explained in the lecture?","['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nJOINT INTENTIONS • Just as we have individual intentions, we can have joint intentions for a team of agents • Levesque defined the idea of a joint persistent goal (JPG). • A group of agents have a collective commitment to bring about some goal φ, “move the couch” • Agents don’t believe that φ is satisfied, but believe it is possible. • Agents maintain the goal φ until a termination condition is reached. 11', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nJOINT INTENTIONS • The termination condition is achieved when an agent realizes that the goal is satisfied, impossible, the motivation is no longer present and so on. • But it doesn’t drop the goal right away • Instead, it adopts a new goal — to make this new knowledge mutually believed → this ensures that the agents are coordinated • They don’t stop working towards the goal until they are all appraised of the situation • Mutual belief is achieved by communication 12', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY • Overall, of the three lectures: • Key assumption: benevolence • Agents are working together, not in competition • In today’s lecture, We discussed a number of ways of having agents decide what to do, and make sure that their work is coordinated 14', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nAGENT ORIENTED METHODOLOGIES • Each methodology may introduce further abstractions - around which to model software and to organize the software process • For example: roles, organizations, responsibilities, belief, desire and intentions • Not directly translating into concrete entities of the software system → the concept of role is an aspect of an agent, not an agent 4', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nINCONSISTENCY • A group of agents may have inconsistencies in their: • Beliefs • Goals or intentions • Inconsistent beliefs arise because agents have different views of the world • May be due to sensor faults or noise or just because they can’t see everything • Inconsistent goals may arise because agents are built by different people with different objectives 10']","Based on the provided context, intentions play a crucial role in practical reasoning in the context of joint intentions. According to the lecture, a group of agents have a collective commitment to bring about some goal φ, ""move the couch"". The agents don't believe that φ is satisfied, but they believe it is possible. They maintain the goal φ until a termination condition is reached.
+
+When the termination condition is achieved, the agent realizes that the goal is satisfied, impossible, the motivation is no longer present, and so on. However, instead of dropping the goal right away, the agent adopts a new goal — to make this new knowledge mutually believed. This ensures that the agents are coordinated and work towards the goal until they are all appraised of the situation.
+
+In essence, intentions are essential in practical reasoning as they provide a shared understanding and commitment among agents to achieve a common goal. They facilitate coordination and cooperation among agents, enabling them to work together effectively towards a shared objective.","Intention poses problems for agents, who need to determine ways of achieving them. Intention provides a 'filter' for adopting other intentions which must not conflict.",1.0,0.8362130247940408,,
+What does the 'side effect or package deal problem' refer to in the context of practical reasoning?,"['[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nCOOPERATIVE DISTRIBUTED PROBLEM SOLVING (CDPS) CPDS addresses the following: 1. Problem decomposition: How can a problem be divided into smaller tasks for distribution amongst agents? 2. Sub-problem solution: • How can the overall problem-solving activities of the agents be optimised so as to produce a solution that maximises the coherence metric? • What techniques can be used to coordinate the activity of the agents, thus avoiding destructive interactions? 3. Solution synthesis:', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nTHE CONTRACT NET VIA FIPA PERFORMATIVES • cfp (call for proposals): Used for announcing a task • propose, refuse: Used for making a proposal, or declining to make a proposal • accept, reject: Used to indicate acceptance or rejection of a proposal • inform, failure: Used to indicate completion of a task (with the result) or failure to do so 10 manager contractor 1 contractor 2 contractor n cfp propose propose propose accept reject reject inform failure OR', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nRESULT SHARING • In results sharing, agents provide each other with information as they work towards a solution • It is generally accepted that results sharing improves problem solving by: • Cross-checking independent pieces of a solution • Combining local views to achieve a better overall view • Sharing results to improve the accuracy of results • Sharing results to allow the use of parallel resources on a problem 14', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nWHERE DOES THIS BRINGS US? • Interconnection and Distribution have become core motifs in Computer Science • But Interconnection and Distribution, coupled with the need for systems to represent our best interests, implies systems that can cooperate and reach agreements (or even compete) with other systems that have different interests (much as we do with other people) 21', '[Course Metadata] Course: BAXI 3113 INTELLIGENT AGENT; Lecturer/Author: Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain; Department: Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA)\n\n---\n\nRESULT SHARING • In results sharing, agents provide each other with information as they work towards a solution • It is generally accepted that results sharing improves problem solving by: • Cross-checking independent pieces of a solution • Combining local views to achieve a better overall view • Sharing results to improve the accuracy of results • Sharing results to allow the use of parallel resources on a problem 3']","Based on the context, the 'side effect or package deal problem' refers to a situation where an agent's action or decision has an unintended consequence or benefit that is not directly related to the original goal or objective. This problem is related to the concept of results sharing, where agents provide each other with information as they work towards a solution, and the benefits of results sharing can sometimes be seen as a 'package deal', where the agent receives benefits that are not directly related to the original goal.
+
+In other words, the 'side effect or package deal problem' is a type of problem that arises when agents share results, and the benefits of sharing can sometimes be seen as a 'side effect' or a 'package deal' that is not directly related to the original goal. This problem is related to the concept of results sharing, which is discussed in the context of Cooperative Distributed Problem Solving (CDPS) and Intelligent Agents.","The side effect or package deal problem refers to the fact that I may believe that going to the dentist involves pain, and I may also intend to go to the dentist — but this does not imply that I intend to suffer pain!",0.16666666666666666,0.8570853231837292,0.0,0.0
