+
+In [S4], it is mentioned that the message condition is matched against the messages the agent has received, and the mental condition is matched against the beliefs of the agent. This implies that communicative actions involve sending messages that can be received by the agent, which is a key distinction from private actions.
+
+Therefore, based on the context, we can conclude that the difference between a private action and a communicative action in AGENT0 is that private actions are externally executed computations, while communicative actions involve sending messages that can be received by the agent.",Actions may be: Private: An externally executed computation; Communicative: Sending messages.,0.5714285714285714,0.980387277843446,0.9999999999,1.0
+What is the primary purpose of practical reasoning according to the lecture?,"['WHAT IS PRACTICAL REASONING? • Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do • Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes (Bratman) • Distinguish practical reasoning from theoretical reasoning – directed towards belief 3', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Describe practical reasoning • Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2', '• Human practical reasoning consists of two activities: • The interplay between beliefs, desires and intentions defines how the model works COMPONENTS OF PRACTICAL REASONING 4 DELIBERATION • deciding what situation, we want to achieve • the outputs of deliberation are intentions MEANS-ENDS REASONING • deciding how to achieve these states of affairs • the outputs of means-ends reasoning are plans', 'SUMMARY • We have define practical reasoning. • We acknowledge how intentions are key part of practical reasoning. • How intention relates to practical reasoning • Intentions are stronger than desire • Next → Means-ends Reasoning/Planning 9', 'IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • 𝒫{Bel} is the power set of beliefs • Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have PROBLEM: • deliberation and means-ends reasoning processes are not instantaneous \uf0df Cost • So the agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world • The world may change in the meantime 11']","According to the context, the primary purpose of practical reasoning is to figure out what to do, which involves weighing conflicting considerations and taking into account what the agent desires, values, and believes.",Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do.,1.0,0.8750197703114244,0.7555555555303703,1.0
+What does Bratman suggest about practical reasoning?,"['WHAT IS PRACTICAL REASONING? • Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do • Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes (Bratman) • Distinguish practical reasoning from theoretical reasoning – directed towards belief 3', 'SUMMARY • We have define practical reasoning. • We acknowledge how intentions are key part of practical reasoning. • How intention relates to practical reasoning • Intentions are stronger than desire • Next → Means-ends Reasoning/Planning 9', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Describe practical reasoning • Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2', '• Human practical reasoning consists of two activities: • The interplay between beliefs, desires and intentions defines how the model works COMPONENTS OF PRACTICAL REASONING 4 DELIBERATION • deciding what situation, we want to achieve • the outputs of deliberation are intentions MEANS-ENDS REASONING • deciding how to achieve these states of affairs • the outputs of means-ends reasoning are plans', 'INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 5. Agents do not believe they will not bring about their intentions. 6. Under certain circumstances, agents believe they will bring about their intentions. 7']","Based on the context, Bratman is mentioned as the source of information about practical reasoning. According to the text, Bratman suggests that practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes.","Bratman suggests that practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes.",1.0,0.8642977564685043,0.9999999999,1.0
+Explain the distinction between practical reasoning and theoretical reasoning as discussed in the lecture.,"['WHAT IS PRACTICAL REASONING? • Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do • Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes (Bratman) • Distinguish practical reasoning from theoretical reasoning – directed towards belief 3', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Describe practical reasoning • Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2', '• Human practical reasoning consists of two activities: • The interplay between beliefs, desires and intentions defines how the model works COMPONENTS OF PRACTICAL REASONING 4 DELIBERATION • deciding what situation, we want to achieve • the outputs of deliberation are intentions MEANS-ENDS REASONING • deciding how to achieve these states of affairs • the outputs of means-ends reasoning are plans', 'IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • 𝒫{Bel} is the power set of beliefs • Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have PROBLEM: • deliberation and means-ends reasoning processes are not instantaneous \uf0df Cost • So the agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world • The world may change in the meantime 11', 'SUMMARY • We have define practical reasoning. • We acknowledge how intentions are key part of practical reasoning. • How intention relates to practical reasoning • Intentions are stronger than desire • Next → Means-ends Reasoning/Planning 9']","According to the lecture, the distinction between practical reasoning and theoretical reasoning is as follows:
+
+Practical reasoning is directed towards actions and is concerned with figuring out what to do. It involves weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, taking into account what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes.
+
