+
+**Summary**: Agents are systems that can act independently, make decisions, and adapt to their surroundings to fulfill delegated objectives, moving beyond simple passive service providers.",The main point about agents is they are autonomous: capable of independent action.,0.875,0.9241201371248963,0.99999999998,1.0
+What types of behaviour does an intelligent agent typically exhibit?,"['INTELLIGENT AGENT It is typically thought as exhibiting 3 types of behaviour: • Reactive • Pro-active • Social Ability 7 An intelligent agent is a computer system capable of flexible autonomous action in some environment', '18 Agents are autonomous: • agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent Agents are smart: • capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour Agents are active: • not passive service providers;', 'BROOKS BEHAVIOURAL LANGUAGES Brooks’ research is based on two key ideas: 1. Situatedness and embodiment: ‘Real’ intelligence is situated in the world, not in disembodied systems such as theorem provers or expert systems. 2. Intelligence and emergence: ‘Intelligent’ behaviour arises as a result of an agent’s interaction with its environment. Also, intelligence is ‘in the eye of the beholder’; it is not an innate, isolated property. 7', 'RECAP • Agent behaviors – Reactive, Pro-active, Social • Properties of environment – Real time, Dynamic, Episodic etc. • Compare agent with objects and AI systems • Agents as intentional system • Tasks for agents – Achievement Task vs Management Task • Abstract architectures for agents 2', 'MODERN EXAMPLES OF INTELLIGENT AGENTS 31 Field Example Education AI tutors & content generators (ChatGPT, Khanmigo) Healthcare Diagnosis & therapy bots (Woebot, Glass Health) Software GitHub Copilot & debug agents Creativity Adobe Firefly, Midjourney, Gemini for design support Planning Autonomous research assistants']","Based on the provided sources and definitions, an intelligent agent typically exhibits the following three types of behavior:
+
+1.  **Reactive Behavior**: The agent responds to specific inputs or triggers immediately, without prior planning or memory of past events. It acts based on the current state of the environment.
+2.  **Pro-Active Behavior**: The agent anticipates future events, potential problems, or opportunities within the environment and takes appropriate action to address them before they become urgent. This involves planning and foresight.
+3.  **Social Behavior**: The agent engages in communication, negotiation, or collaboration with other agents and entities within the environment. This often involves recognizing the needs of others and initiating interaction to achieve common goals.
+
+These three behaviors are highlighted in the context as the fundamental characteristics of a flexible, autonomous system capable of complex environmental interaction, aligning with the standard object-oriented model and the emergent nature of intelligence.","An intelligent agent is a computer system capable of flexible autonomous action in some environment. It exhibits 3 types of behaviour: reactive, pro-active, and social ability.",0.1111111111111111,0.935447636748453,0.9166666666361111,1.0
+Why is a reactive system considered easy to build for fixed environments?,"['REACTIVITY • Software is hard to build for dynamic domains: program must consider possibility of failure • A reactive system is one that maintains an ongoing interaction with its environment and responds to changes that occur in it (in time for the response to be useful). 9', 'LIMITATIONS OF REACTIVE SYSTEMS • As actions are based on local information, such agents inherently take a “short-term” view • Emergent behaviour is very hard to engineer or validate; typically a trial and error approach is ultimately adopted • Whilst agents with few layers are straightforward to build, models using many layers are inherently complex and difficult to understand \uf0df Design approach does not scale-up for large systems 11', 'ADVANTAGES OF REACTIVE SYSTEMS • Simplicity, i.e. modules have high expressiveness • Computational tractability • Robustness against failure, i.e. possibility of modeling redundancies • Overall behavior emerges from interactions 10', 'REACTIVITY • If a program’s environment is guaranteed to be fixed, the program need never worry about its own success or failure • Program just executes blindly. • Example of fixed environment: compiler. • The real world is not like that: most environments are dynamic and information is incomplete. 8', 'HYBRID ARCHITECTURES • Idea: Neither completely deliberative nor completely reactive architectures are suitable → combine both perspectives in one hybrid architecture • Build an agent out of two (or more) subsystems: • a deliberative one: responsible for abstract planning and decision-making using symbolic representations • a reactive one: capable to respond to world changes without any complex reasoning and decision-making 3']","Based on the provided context and the **S4** document, here is the analysis regarding why a reactive system is considered easy to build for fixed environments:
+
+### Analysis based on the Document
+
+**Source: S4 (L2.0 - Intelligent Agents (1).pdf, page 8)**
+**Title: REACTIVITY • If a program's environment is guaranteed to be fixed, the program need never worry about its own success or failure • Program just executes blindly.**
+
+The text explicitly defines the relationship between a **fixed environment** and a **reactive system**:
+
+1.  **""If a program's environment is guaranteed to be fixed""**: This is the primary condition mentioned for the ease of building a reactive system.
+2.  **""Program just executes blindly""**: When the environment is fixed, there is no need to model potential failures, plan long-term strategies, or handle incomplete information. The system relies solely on the rules and current state provided by the environment.
+3.  **""Example of fixed environment: compiler""**: The compiler is a classic example of a fixed system. A compiler has a predictable input, a known set of rules, and a known output, so it does not experience runtime failures or complex logic errors.
+
+### Conclusion
+
+A reactive system is considered easy to build for fixed environments because **it eliminates the need for reasoning and planning**. The definition of a reactive system (maintaining ongoing interaction) is most straightforward when the environment is already known to be static and complete, meaning the agent has no uncertainty to model.
+
+In simpler terms: If the environment is guaranteed to be fixed, you don't need to worry about ""what if"" scenarios, and the agent simply follows the instructions given by the environment to execute.","If a program’s environment is guaranteed to be fixed, the program need never worry about its own success or failure. The real world is not like that: most environments are dynamic and information is incomplete.",0.28,0.9245571488642952,0.249999999975,1.0
+What does proactiveness involve for agents?,"['PROACTIVENESS • Reacting to an environment is easy • e.g., stimulus → response rules • But we generally want agents to do things for us • Hence goal directed behaviour • Pro-activeness: generating and attempting to achieve goals; not driven solely by events; taking the initiative and recognising opportunities 10', 'AGENT ARCHITECTURES Three broad categories: 4 Deliberative Architectures focus on planning and symbolic reasoning Reactive Architectures focus on reactivity based on behavioural rules Hybrid Architectures attempt to balance proactiveness with reactivity', '18 Agents are autonomous: • agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent Agents are smart: • capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour Agents are active: • not passive service providers;', 'RECAP • Agent behaviors – Reactive, Pro-active, Social • Properties of environment – Real time, Dynamic, Episodic etc. • Compare agent with objects and AI systems • Agents as intentional system • Tasks for agents – Achievement Task vs Management Task • Abstract architectures for agents 2', 'RECOGNITION • In this stage, an agent recognises it has a problem it needs help with • Agent has a goal, and either • realises it cannot achieve the goal in isolation - i.e. it does not have capability • realises it would prefer not to achieve the goal in isolation (typically because of solution quality, deadline etc.) • As a result, it needs to involve other agents 6']","Based on the provided context and the definitions from the sources, **proactiveness involves an agent actively generating and attempting to achieve goals.**
+
+It goes beyond simple reaction to events (reactive behavior). Instead, proactiveness requires the agent to take initiative, identify opportunities, and work toward specific objectives. In the context of the *Intelligent Agent* (S1), proactiveness is defined as ""generating and attempting to achieve goals; not driven solely by events.""",Pro-activeness: generating and attempting to achieve goals; not driven solely by events; taking the initiative and recognising opportunities.,0.8333333333333334,0.7601628723551753,0.9999999999,1.0
+"What is the difference between objects and agents, and how do agents differ from objects?","['AGENTS AND OBJECTS Are agents just objects by another name? • Object: • encapsulates some state; • communicates via message passing; • has methods, corresponding to operations that • may be performed on this state. 17', '18 Agents are autonomous: • agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent Agents are smart: • capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour Agents are active: • not passive service providers;', 'AGENTS: A FIRST DEFINITION • An agent is a computer system that is capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of its user or owner • I.e., figuring out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives, rather than constantly being told • Modern agents may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming 24', 'RECAP • Agent behaviors – Reactive, Pro-active, Social • Properties of environment – Real time, Dynamic, Episodic etc. • Compare agent with objects and AI systems • Agents as intentional system • Tasks for agents – Achievement Task vs Management Task • Abstract architectures for agents 2', 'METATEM AGENTS • A Concurrent MetateM system contains a number of agents (objects) • Each object has 3 attributes: • a name • an interface • a MetateM program 32 • An agent’s interface contains two sets: • messages the agent will accept • messages the agent may send']","Based on the provided context, here is the differentiation between **objects** and **agents**:
+
+### 1. Definition of an Object
+An **object** is a self-contained entity that encapsulates specific **state** and **behavioral capabilities**.
+*   **Key Characteristics:**
+    *   Encapsulates state: It holds data relevant to its function.
+    *   Communicates via message passing: It acts as a container that can trigger actions on the environment.
+    *   Has methods: It defines specific operations (functions) that can be performed when interacting with the environment.
+
+### 2. Definition of an Agent
+An **agent** is a distinct system (computer system) designed to **act autonomously** to achieve specific goals.
+*   **Key Characteristics:**
+    *   Embodies a stronger **autonomy**: It makes decisions and performs actions on its own, rather than passively waiting for instructions.
+    *   Decides whether to perform an action: Unlike an object, an agent evaluates whether the request it received requires its own execution.
+    *   Uses generative AI (reactive, proactive, social): Agents can infer needs from context, create solutions, or engage in social interaction without constant manual programming.
+    *   Is an **intentional system**: It has a clear purpose and owns its own workflow.
+
+### Summary Comparison
+| Feature | Object | Agent |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| **Nature** | A self-contained entity (like a program) | A system of entities (like an intelligent agent) |
+| **State** | Holds specific data and methods | Holds state *and* the ability to act |
+| **Decision** | Passive (gets told to do something) | Active (decides on its own) |
+| **Flexibility** | Reactive or Proactive depending on the object | Flexible (reactive, proactive, social) |
+| **Primary Role** | To manage resources or data | To solve problems autonomously |
+
+**Conclusion:**
+In the context of the provided text, an **object** is simply the ""container"" holding data and methods (like a function or a program instance), while an **agent** is a specialized system that owns that container and uses it to make autonomous decisions, solve problems, and communicate dynamically.","Agents are autonomous: agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent. Agents are smart: agents are capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour. Agents are active: agents are not passive service providers; a multi-agent system is inherently multi-threaded, in that each agent is assumed to have at least one thread of active control. Objects do it because they have to! Objects do it for free! Agents do it because they want to! Agents do it for personal gain!",,0.8967922856656135,0.49999999995,0.5
+What was the classical approach to building agents known as?,"['SYMBOLIC REASONING AGENTS • The classical approach to building agents is to view them as a particular type of knowledge-based system and bring all the associated methodologies of such systems to bear. • This paradigm is known as symbolic AI • A deliberative agent or agent architecture is defined to be one that: • contains an explicitly represented, symbolic model of the world; • makes decisions (for example about what actions to perform) via symbolic reasoning. 9', 'SUMMARY • Defined the main agent architectures and symbolic reasoning approach • Discussed how logic enables deductive reasoning in agents • Introduced agent-oriented programming (Agent0, PLACA, Concurrent METATEM) • Connected classical reasoning agents to modern Agentic AI systems 35', 'RELEVANT METHODOLOGIES • GAIA Methodologies • Encourages a developer to think of building agent-based systems as a process of organisational design • Agent UML • Extend Unified Modeling Language (UML) with agent features • Prometeus • Focuses mainly on the internal agent architecture → basically a methodology for designing BDI agent systems 7', 'LIMITATIONS OF REACTIVE SYSTEMS • As actions are based on local information, such agents inherently take a “short-term” view • Emergent behaviour is very hard to engineer or validate; typically a trial and error approach is ultimately adopted • Whilst agents with few layers are straightforward to build, models using many layers are inherently complex and difficult to understand \uf0df Design approach does not scale-up for large systems 11', 'INTELLIGENT AGENTS AND AI • When building an agent, we simply want a system that can choose the right action to perform, typically in a limited domain • We do not have to solve all the problems of AI to build a useful agent → a little intelligence goes a long way! 20 “We made our agents dumber and dumber and dumber . . . until finally they made money.” - Oren Etzioni regarding NETBOT, Inc.']","The classical approach to building agents known as was **symbolic AI**.
