+"What does the evolution towards agentic AI bring us, according to the lecture?","['EVOLUTION TOWARD AGENTIC AI Era Agent Type Key Feature Example 1990s Reactive Respond to stimuli Subsumption robot 2000s Deliberative Plan before acting BDI model (Poole & Mackworth Ch. 4) 2010s Cognitive Learn & reason IBM Watson 2020s Agentic AI Use LLMs to understand, plan & act ChatGPT Agents, AutoGPT 22', 'AGENTIC AI • Agentic AI refers to AI systems that can act independently, make decisions, and perform tasks to achieve specific goals. • These systems combine reasoning, memory, and generative abilities to plan and adapt their actions. • Unlike traditional AI, they don’t just respond — they take initiative and collaborate with humans or other agents. 26', 'INTENTIONAL STANCE IN TODAY’S AI • LLMs and agentic AI often seem to make choices or have goals • When we say “ChatGPT understands” or “the agent decides,” we’re using the intentional stance • These systems don’t truly think, but this view helps us explain and predict their behaviour • Talking about AI “as if it thinks” makes human–AI interaction easier to understand. 37', 'SYMBOLIC AI - CRITICS • Recall symbolic AI in Deductive Reasoning Agent lecture. • Is inference on symbols representing the world sufficient to solve real-world problems? • Problems with “symbolic AI”: • Computational complexity of reasoning in real-world applications • The transduction/knowledge acquisition bottleneck • Logic-based approaches largely focus on theoretical reasoning • Detached from interaction with physical world 3', 'architectures attempt to combine the best of reasoning and reactive architectures 2012–present: Agentic AI • Agents that combine reasoning with LLMs to plan, act, and collaborate autonomously']","Based on the provided lecture excerpts, the evolution towards **Agentic AI** brings us a fundamental shift in how we conceptualize and interact with artificial intelligence.
+
+Here is a breakdown of what the text suggests this evolution brings to our understanding of AI:
+
+### 1. From ""Reactive"" to ""Intentional""
+*   **1990s:** Reactive robots and software respond to stimuli immediately (like a thermostat turning on or a robot moving).
+*   **2000s:** Deliberative planning (similar to the **PDI** model) allowed software to plan a route before acting, showing a form of **intentionality**.
+*   **2020s:** The shift points to **Agentic AI** (explicitly defined in **S2**). In this era, AI is described as systems that ""take initiative"" and ""collaborate."" This means the AI isn't just executing a pre-written script; it is making a conscious decision (""I will do this"") and executing it, which requires higher-level reasoning and planning.
+
+### 2. The Rise of Collaboration and Agency
+*   **Key Feature:** The text emphasizes that these systems do not just respond; they **act independently**, decide, and **collaborate** with humans or other agents.
+*   **Implication:** The change is moving from the *outcome* being predetermined to the *process* being active. This highlights that the future of AI lies in the ability to work collaboratively across contexts rather than operating as isolated tools.
+
+### 3. Breaking Down Complexity
+*   **Source S4 (Symbolic AI Critique):** It highlights that while symbolic AI can reason about complex problems, real-world application suffers from **transduction** (the gap between logic and reality).
+*   **S3 (Intentional Stance):** This suggests the future of AI will rely on this ""intentional stance""—the belief that AI should understand goals rather than just processing data. This allows us to predict behavior better than the old reactive models.
+
+### 4. Combining Reasoning with Action
+*   **Source S5 (Architectures):** The text notes that modern architectures aim to combine **reasoning** (logic) with **reactive** (decision-making) capabilities. In the agentic era, this is achieved through combining reasoning with **LLMs** to plan, act, and collaborate autonomously.
+
+### Summary
+According to the lecture, the evolution towards Agentic AI brings us the realization that **AI is moving from simple task execution to complex decision-making and proactive collaboration.** It challenges the notion that AI merely processes data; instead, it is increasingly capable of understanding goals, making autonomous choices, and adapting to new challenges in real-time.","The ability of computer systems to act independently, the ability of computer systems to act in a way that represents our best interests while interacting with other humans or systems",1.0,0.6505570103998424,0.94999999997625,1.0
