+What is the main point about agents being autonomous?,"['WHAT IS AN AGENT? • The main point about agents is they are autonomous: capable independent action 4 An agent is a computer system that is situated in some environment, and that is capable of autonomous action in that environment in order to meet its delegated objectives • It is all about decisions: • An agent must choose what action to perform • An agent must decide when to perform an action', 'WORKING TOGETHER • Since agents are autonomous, they have to make decisions at run-time, and be capable of dynamic coordination • Overall, they will need to be able to share: • Tasks • Information • No common goals if agents designed by different individuals • Important to make a distinction between: • benevolent agents and • self-interested agents 3', '18 Agents are autonomous: • agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent Agents are smart: • capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour Agents are active: • not passive service providers;', 'AUTONOMOUS • By autonomous mobile, we mean agents that are able to decide for themselves where to go, when, and what to do when they get there (subject to certain resource constraints) • Such agents are generally programmed in a special language that provides a go instruction • best known example is TELESCRIPT 12', 'AGENTS: A FIRST DEFINITION • An agent is a computer system that is capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of its user or owner • I.e., figuring out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives, rather than constantly being told • Modern agents may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming 24']","The main point about agents being autonomous is that they are **capable of independent, autonomous action** within their defined environment to meet specific objectives.",The main point about agents is they are autonomous: capable of independent action.,,0.0,0.99999999998,1.0
+What types of behaviour does an intelligent agent typically exhibit?,"['INTELLIGENT AGENT It is typically thought as exhibiting 3 types of behaviour: • Reactive • Pro-active • Social Ability 7 An intelligent agent is a computer system capable of flexible autonomous action in some environment', '18 Agents are autonomous: • agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent Agents are smart: • capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour Agents are active: • not passive service providers;', 'BROOKS BEHAVIOURAL LANGUAGES Brooks’ research is based on two key ideas: 1. Situatedness and embodiment: ‘Real’ intelligence is situated in the world, not in disembodied systems such as theorem provers or expert systems. 2. Intelligence and emergence: ‘Intelligent’ behaviour arises as a result of an agent’s interaction with its environment. Also, intelligence is ‘in the eye of the beholder’; it is not an innate, isolated property. 7', 'RECAP • Agent behaviors – Reactive, Pro-active, Social • Properties of environment – Real time, Dynamic, Episodic etc. • Compare agent with objects and AI systems • Agents as intentional system • Tasks for agents – Achievement Task vs Management Task • Abstract architectures for agents 2', 'MODERN EXAMPLES OF INTELLIGENT AGENTS 31 Field Example Education AI tutors & content generators (ChatGPT, Khanmigo) Healthcare Diagnosis & therapy bots (Woebot, Glass Health) Software GitHub Copilot & debug agents Creativity Adobe Firefly, Midjourney, Gemini for design support Planning Autonomous research assistants']","Based on the context provided, an intelligent agent typically exhibits **three** distinct types of behaviour:
