+3. An example is when an agent identifies a problem it cannot solve on its own (e.g., a user's issue) and instead delegates the task to another agent, demonstrating pro-activeness.
+
+In summary, proactiveness requires agents to **make proactive decisions and take initiative** to achieve their objectives, rather than just reacting to immediate events.",Pro-activeness: generating and attempting to achieve goals; not driven solely by events; taking the initiative and recognising opportunities.,1.0,0.0,0.8041666666465626,1.0
+"What is the difference between objects and agents, and how do agents differ from objects?","['AGENTS AND OBJECTS Are agents just objects by another name? • Object: • encapsulates some state; • communicates via message passing; • has methods, corresponding to operations that • may be performed on this state. 17', '18 Agents are autonomous: • agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent Agents are smart: • capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour Agents are active: • not passive service providers;', 'AGENTS: A FIRST DEFINITION • An agent is a computer system that is capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of its user or owner • I.e., figuring out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives, rather than constantly being told • Modern agents may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming 24', 'RECAP • Agent behaviors – Reactive, Pro-active, Social • Properties of environment – Real time, Dynamic, Episodic etc. • Compare agent with objects and AI systems • Agents as intentional system • Tasks for agents – Achievement Task vs Management Task • Abstract architectures for agents 2', 'METATEM AGENTS • A Concurrent MetateM system contains a number of agents (objects) • Each object has 3 attributes: • a name • an interface • a MetateM program 32 • An agent’s interface contains two sets: • messages the agent will accept • messages the agent may send']","Based on the provided context, here is a breakdown of the relationship between objects and agents, their definitions, and the key differences highlighted.
+
+### 1. Definitions and Analogies
+
+The context provides several definitions to clarify the relationship:
+
+*   **From [S1]:** ""AGENTS... are just objects... encapsulates some state; communicates via message passing; has methods...""
+    *   *Interpretation:* In this specific context, the term **""object""** refers to the specific, concrete entity that holds the state. It is the static, well-defined package of data (state) and communication rules defined at the programming level. An object is a physical or logical entity defined by its state and communication protocols.
+
+*   **From [S2]:** ""Agents are autonomous... embody a stronger notion of autonomy...""
+    *   *Interpretation:* The term **""agent""** refers to the *functionality* or the system that *acts* on behalf of a user. It is the dynamic, evolving entity capable of independent action. An agent is the program (or system) that decides what to do, whereas an object is the state (data) held by that program.
+
+*   **From [S3]:** ""An agent is a computer system that is capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of its user...""
+    *   *Interpretation:* This confirms that **agents** are the functional units or software systems that perform tasks. The **objects** mentioned in [S1] are the *state* or *data* associated with these systems. The system acts on the object, not the other way around.
+
+### 2. Key Differences
+
+The text explicitly outlines how they differ through specific capabilities:
+
+| Feature | Object | Agent |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| **Nature** | A static, data container with defined properties (Name, Interface, State). | An autonomous system capable of independent action. |
+| **Action** | Acts based on instructions or data provided. | Decides itself (autonomous) what to do. |
+| **Communication** | Communicates via **message passing**. | Communicates via **message passing** (as part of its autonomous behavior). |
+| **Behavior** | **Not** flexible or social; it follows standard object-oriented rules (reactive, pro-active). | **Is** flexible (reactive, pro-active, or social). It adapts context and creates its own solutions. |
+| **Status** | Passive service provider of a specific state. | Active service provider (autonomous) on behalf of a user. |
+
+### 3. Summary Conclusion
+
+In this context:
+*   **Objects** are the **state** or **data package** defined at the program level (e.g., `UserState`, `ConfigurationObject`). They hold the ""what"" to communicate about.
+*   **Agents** are the **systems** (software or hardware) that perform the **action**. They possess the ""who"" and the ""how"" to decide what to do independently of external programming.
+
+**Crucial Distinction:** An agent is the agent **itself** (the program), while an object is the **object** it contains. You cannot talk about an object without mentioning an agent, and an agent cannot talk about an object unless it has been configured to do so via a message passing protocol.","Agents are autonomous: agents embody stronger notion of autonomy than objects, and in particular, they decide for themselves whether or not to perform an action on request from another agent. Agents are smart: agents are capable of flexible (reactive, pro-active, social) behaviour – the standard object-oriented model has nothing to say about such types of behaviour. Agents are active: agents are not passive service providers; a multi-agent system is inherently multi-threaded, in that each agent is assumed to have at least one thread of active control. Objects do it because they have to! Objects do it for free! Agents do it because they want to! Agents do it for personal gain!",0.0,0.8608057362506111,0.99999999998,1.0
+What was the classical approach to building agents known as?,"['SYMBOLIC REASONING AGENTS • The classical approach to building agents is to view them as a particular type of knowledge-based system and bring all the associated methodologies of such systems to bear. • This paradigm is known as symbolic AI • A deliberative agent or agent architecture is defined to be one that: • contains an explicitly represented, symbolic model of the world; • makes decisions (for example about what actions to perform) via symbolic reasoning. 9', 'SUMMARY • Defined the main agent architectures and symbolic reasoning approach • Discussed how logic enables deductive reasoning in agents • Introduced agent-oriented programming (Agent0, PLACA, Concurrent METATEM) • Connected classical reasoning agents to modern Agentic AI systems 35', 'RELEVANT METHODOLOGIES • GAIA Methodologies • Encourages a developer to think of building agent-based systems as a process of organisational design • Agent UML • Extend Unified Modeling Language (UML) with agent features • Prometeus • Focuses mainly on the internal agent architecture → basically a methodology for designing BDI agent systems 7', 'LIMITATIONS OF REACTIVE SYSTEMS • As actions are based on local information, such agents inherently take a “short-term” view • Emergent behaviour is very hard to engineer or validate; typically a trial and error approach is ultimately adopted • Whilst agents with few layers are straightforward to build, models using many layers are inherently complex and difficult to understand \uf0df Design approach does not scale-up for large systems 11', 'INTELLIGENT AGENTS AND AI • When building an agent, we simply want a system that can choose the right action to perform, typically in a limited domain • We do not have to solve all the problems of AI to build a useful agent → a little intelligence goes a long way! 20 “We made our agents dumber and dumber and dumber . . . until finally they made money.” - Oren Etzioni regarding NETBOT, Inc.']","The classical approach to building agents is known as symbolic AI (or **Symbolic Reasoning AI**).
+
+In the provided context:
