+*   **[S2]** mentions the ""symbolic reasoning approach"" which connects to the classical definition of symbolic AI.
+*   **[S3]** introduces ""Agent UML"" and ""Agent-oriented programming,"" which are components of the classical approach.
+
+Therefore, the specific name used to describe this tradition is **Symbolic AI**.",The classical approach to building agents was known as symbolic AI.,,0.6884647583583626,0.99999999998,0.6
+"According to the lecture, what is the underlying problem with knowledge representation and reasoning?","['THE REPRESENTATION / REASONING PROBLEM • The underlying problem with knowledge representation / reasoning lies with the complexity of symbol manipulation algorithms. • In general many (most) search-based symbol manipulation algorithms of interest are highly intractable. • Hard to find compact representations. • Because of these problems, some researchers have looked to alternative techniques for building agents; we look at these later. 11', 'TWO ISSUES 10 The Transduction Problem The transduction problem is that of translating the real world into an accurate, adequate symbolic description, in time for that description to be useful. This has led onto research into vision, speech understanding, learning… The Representation / Reasoning Problem How to symbolically represent information about complex real-world entities and processes, and how to get agents to reason with this information in time for the results to be useful.', 'SYMBOLIC AI - CRITICS • Recall symbolic AI in Deductive Reasoning Agent lecture. • Is inference on symbols representing the world sufficient to solve real-world problems? • Problems with “symbolic AI”: • Computational complexity of reasoning in real-world applications • The transduction/knowledge acquisition bottleneck • Logic-based approaches largely focus on theoretical reasoning • Detached from interaction with physical world 3', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Explain how means-ends reasoning fit into the basic agent control loop • Representation • Relate blocksworld to the planning problem • Practical reasoning agent implementation • Deliberation 2', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Describe practical reasoning • Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2']","Based on the lecture notes and the provided PDFs, here is the breakdown of the underlying problems identified in the text:
+
+1.  **Highly Intractable Symbol Manipulation Algorithms**: The text explicitly states that general search-based algorithms for manipulating symbols are ""highly intractable.""
+2.  **Hard to find Compact Representations**: There is difficulty in creating simple, compact, or efficient representations of information about complex real-world entities.
+3.  **The ""Transduction Problem""**: This is described as the specific challenge of translating the real world into an accurate, adequate symbolic description in time for that description to be useful.
+
+### Conclusion
+
+According to the lecture, the underlying problem with knowledge representation and reasoning is primarily defined by three key issues:
+*   **The Intractable Nature of Symbol Manipulation Algorithms**: Most search-based algorithms are too complex and computationally expensive.
+*   **The Complexity of Representation**: It is difficult to create compact or efficient symbolic representations of complex real-world data.
+*   **The Specific Bottleneck: Transduction**: There is a specific difficulty in translating the actual, messy real-world world into a clear and useful symbolic model, often referred to as the ""Transduction Problem.""",The underlying problem with knowledge representation / reasoning lies with the complexity of symbol manipulation algorithms.,1.0,0.8938856547914616,0.999999999975,1.0
+What is the key idea of agent-oriented programming as introduced by Yoav Shoham?,"['AGENT-ORIENTED PROGRAMMING • Yoav Shoham introduced “agent-oriented programming” in 1990: “... new programming paradigm, based on a societal view of computation ...” • The key idea: • Directly programming agents in terms of intentional notions • like belief, desire, and intention • Adopts the same abstraction as humans • Resulted in the Agent0 programming language 21', 'SUMMARY • Defined the main agent architectures and symbolic reasoning approach • Discussed how logic enables deductive reasoning in agents • Introduced agent-oriented programming (Agent0, PLACA, Concurrent METATEM) • Connected classical reasoning agents to modern Agentic AI systems 35', 'SUMMARY • In this lecture, we explored why traditional object-oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi-agent systems. • We introduced agent-oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. • We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent-based analysis and design. • We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23', 'AGENT ORIENTED METHODOLOGIES • Each methodology may introduce further abstractions - around which to model software and to organize the software process • For example: roles, organizations, responsibilities, belief, desire and intentions • Not directly translating into concrete entities of the software system → the concept of role is an aspect of an agent, not an agent 4', 'AGENTS AS INTENTIONAL SYSTEMS 3. Post-Declarative Systems • In procedural programming, we say exactly what a system should do; • In declarative programming, we state something that we want to achieve, give the system general info about the relationships between objects, and let a built-in control mechanism (e. g. , goal-directed theorem proving) figure out what to do;']","The key idea of agent-oriented programming, as introduced by Yoav Shoham, is to **program agents directly in terms of intentional notions** such as belief, desire, and intention. This approach adopts an abstract model similar to humans, allowing for a more natural and intuitive way to represent the decisions and actions of agents.","The key idea of agent-oriented programming as introduced by Yoav Shoham is to directly program agents in terms of intentional notions like belief, desire, and intention, and to adopt the same abstraction as humans.",1.0,0.9634587368089998,0.94999999997625,0.5
+What is the key component of an agent in AGENT0 that determines how the agent acts?,"['AGENT0 • The key component, which determines how the agent acts, is the commitment rule set. • Each commitment rule contains • a message condition; • a mental condition; and • an action 23', 'AGENT0 • AGENT0 is implemented as an extension to LISP. • Each agent in AGENT0 has 4 components: • a set of capabilities (things the agent can do); • a set of initial beliefs; • a set of initial commitments (things the agent will do); and • a set of commitment rules. 22', 'WHAT IS AN AGENT? • The main point about agents is they are autonomous: capable independent action 4 An agent is a computer system that is situated in some environment, and that is capable of autonomous action in that environment in order to meet its delegated objectives • It is all about decisions: • An agent must choose what action to perform • An agent must decide when to perform an action', 'NEGOTIATION SETTING Any negotiation setting will have four components: 1. A negotiation set: possible proposals that agents can make 2. A protocol: which defines what legal proposals an agent can make, based on prior negotiations 3. A collection of strategies, one for each agent, which are private 4. A rule that determines when a deal has been struck and what the agreement deal is 22', 'AGENTS: A FIRST DEFINITION • An agent is a computer system that is capable of independent (autonomous) action on behalf of its user or owner • I.e., figuring out what needs to be done to satisfy design objectives, rather than constantly being told • Modern agents may use generative AI models to reason, adapt to context, and create solutions without explicit re-programming 24']",The key component of an agent in AGENT0 that determines how the agent acts is the **commitment rule set**.,"The key component, which determines how the agent acts, is the commitment rule set.",,0.9999999999999621,0.99999999998,1.0
+"In AGENT0, what is the difference between a private action and a communicative action?","['AGENT0 DECISION CYCLE • Actions may be: • Private: An externally executed computation • Communicative: Sending messages • Messages are constrained to be one of three types 1. Requests: To commit to action 2. Unrequests: To refrain from action 3. Informs: Which pass on information 25', 'PLACA • A more refined implementation was developed by Becky Thomas, for her 1993 doctoral thesis. • Her Planning Communicating Agents (PLACA) language was intended to address one severe drawback to AGENT0 - the inability of agents to plan and communicate requests for action via high-level goals • Agents in PLACA are programmed in much the same way as in AGENT0, in terms of mental change rules. 28', 'WHAT IS AN AGENT? • The main point about agents is they are autonomous: capable independent action 4 An agent is a computer system that is situated in some environment, and that is capable of autonomous action in that environment in order to meet its delegated objectives • It is all about decisions: • An agent must choose what action to perform • An agent must decide when to perform an action', 'AGENTS WITH STATE - PERCEPT 40 The see function is the agent’s ability to observe its environment, whereas the action function represents the agent’s decision-making process. Output of the see function is a percept: ...which maps environment states to percepts.', 'AGENT COMMUNICATION LANGUAGES 10']","In the context of **AGENT0**, the distinction between a **private action** and a **communicative action** arises from how the agent interacts with its environment and how information is exchanged.
+
+*   **Private Action**: This is an **externally executed computation**. An agent performing a private action does not send any information to another entity (like a peer or another agent) nor does it send messages. The agent simply runs a program locally in its own computing environment to achieve a specific result. It operates independently of direct communication with other agents.
+*   **Communicative Action**: This is an **internal action**, defined as sending **messages** (such as requests, requests for information, or updates). To communicate, the agent must send a message to another entity (or itself in some recursive loop) to share the necessary information or state changes. Since the agent is sending messages, it is engaging in communication.
+
+**Summary:**
+The key difference is that a **private action** is a self-contained program executed without external interaction, while a **communicative action** involves the explicit **sending of messages** to facilitate interaction or information exchange.",Actions may be: Private: An externally executed computation; Communicative: Sending messages.,0.5,0.961280375403862,0.99999999998,0.5
+What is the primary purpose of practical reasoning according to the lecture?,"['WHAT IS PRACTICAL REASONING? • Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do • Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes (Bratman) • Distinguish practical reasoning from theoretical reasoning – directed towards belief 3', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Describe practical reasoning • Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Explain how means-ends reasoning fit into the basic agent control loop • Representation • Relate blocksworld to the planning problem • Practical reasoning agent implementation • Deliberation 2', 'IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • 𝒫{Bel} is the power set of beliefs • Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have PROBLEM: • deliberation and means-ends reasoning processes are not instantaneous \uf0df Cost • So the agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world • The world may change in the meantime 11', 'SUMMARY • This lecture looked at argumentation as a means through which agents can reach agreement • Argumentation allows for more complex interactions • Argumentation can be used for a range of tasks that include negotiation • It also allows for inquiry, persuasion, deliberation 13']","Based on the lecture notes and problem statements provided, the primary purpose of practical reasoning is to guide an agent toward **optimal action** by weighing conflicting considerations.
+
