+What does Bratman suggest about practical reasoning?,"['WHAT IS PRACTICAL REASONING? • Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do • Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes (Bratman) • Distinguish practical reasoning from theoretical reasoning – directed towards belief 3', 'SUMMARY • We have define practical reasoning. • We acknowledge how intentions are key part of practical reasoning. • How intention relates to practical reasoning • Intentions are stronger than desire • Next → Means-ends Reasoning/Planning 9', 'IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • 𝒫{Bel} is the power set of beliefs • Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have PROBLEM: • deliberation and means-ends reasoning processes are not instantaneous \uf0df Cost • So the agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world • The world may change in the meantime 11', 'IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • See is as before: • Instead of the function next, which took a percept and used it to update the internal state of an agent, we have a belief revision function 10', 'PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS: COMMITMENT STRATEGIES BAXI 3113 – INTELLIGENT AGENT Dr. Nur Zareen Zulkarnain Department of Intelligent Computing and Analytics (ICA) DG/1-18 zareen@utem.edu.my 1']","Based on the text provided, specifically **[S1]** which defines practical reasoning as ""the process of figuring out what to do"" and distinguishes it from theoretical reasoning (which is directed towards belief), here is a breakdown of what Bratman suggests:
+
+**1. It is an action-oriented process (Action):**
+Bratman defines practical reasoning as directed towards **actions**. It is not a theoretical exercise in analyzing the world's facts; rather, it is the mental process of determining the next step to take in that action.
+
+**2. It involves weighing conflicting considerations:**
+Practical reasoning is characterized by **weighing conflicting considerations**. The agent must evaluate options against competing factors such as:
+*   **Desired values/cares:** What the agent wants to achieve or feel.
+*   **Beliefs:** What the agent believes about the world (which often comes from experience or other agents).
+
+**3. It relies on the agent's desires and values:**
+The text explicitly states: ""Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about."" Therefore, the agent's internal state (desires, values, care) is the primary driver of the decision-making process, not external data.
+
+**In summary:**
+Bratman suggests that practical reasoning is not about understanding what is *true* about the universe (theoretical reasoning), but about **acting** (doing) based on the agent's own **desires, values, and beliefs**. It is a decision-making process where the agent actively weighs options against their own internal goals and perceptions.","Bratman suggests that practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes.",1.0,0.914173024642495,0.94999999997625,1.0
+Explain the distinction between practical reasoning and theoretical reasoning as discussed in the lecture.,"['WHAT IS PRACTICAL REASONING? • Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do • Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes (Bratman) • Distinguish practical reasoning from theoretical reasoning – directed towards belief 3', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Describe practical reasoning • Relate practical reasoning with intentions 2', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Explain how means-ends reasoning fit into the basic agent control loop • Representation • Relate blocksworld to the planning problem • Practical reasoning agent implementation • Deliberation 2', 'IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • 𝒫{Bel} is the power set of beliefs • Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have PROBLEM: • deliberation and means-ends reasoning processes are not instantaneous \uf0df Cost • So the agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world • The world may change in the meantime 11', 'SYMBOLIC AI - CRITICS • Recall symbolic AI in Deductive Reasoning Agent lecture. • Is inference on symbols representing the world sufficient to solve real-world problems? • Problems with “symbolic AI”: • Computational complexity of reasoning in real-world applications • The transduction/knowledge acquisition bottleneck • Logic-based approaches largely focus on theoretical reasoning • Detached from interaction with physical world 3']","Based on the provided lecture materials, here is an explanation of the distinction between **practical reasoning** and **theoretical reasoning**:
+
+### Core Definitions
+*   **Practical Reasoning** is explicitly defined as reasoning directed towards **actions**. It involves weighing conflicting options based on the agent's current desires, values, and beliefs to decide what to do.
+*   **Theoretical Reasoning** is directed towards **beliefs**. It involves forming and updating mental models or assumptions about the world to understand how it works, rather than executing specific tasks.
+
+### Key Distinctions
+1.  **Goal Orientation**:
+    *   *Practical Reasoning*: Goal-oriented. The goal is to ""figure out what to do"" (execute).
+    *   *Theoretical Reasoning*: Goal-less. The goal is to ""think"" about the world or understand its structure.
+
+2.  **Input Sources**:
+    *   *Practical Reasoning*: Relies on the agent's internal state: **""What do I want?""**, **""What do I care about?""**, and **""What do I believe?""**.
+    *   *Theoretical Reasoning*: Relies on external data and abstract representation (**""What is the world actually?""**), but not necessarily the specific goals of the agent.
+
+3.  **Process Nature**:
+    *   *Practical Reasoning*: Dynamic and immediate. An agent observes the world and instantly calculates the best course of action. It deals with real-time constraints (the world may change).
+    *   *Theoretical Reasoning*: Static or delayed. It involves analyzing large sets of facts or symbols to derive a general truth. It often involves deduction or logic, not necessarily action execution.
+
+### Summary
+In short, **practical reasoning** is like having a GPS (you decide *where* to go based on current conditions), while **theoretical reasoning** is like a map (you decide *how* to get there based on the geography).","Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do, while theoretical reasoning is directed towards belief.",1.0,0.0,0.99999999998,1.0
+"What is the role of intentions in practical reasoning, as explained in the lecture?","['INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 1. Intentions pose problems for agents, who need to determine ways of achieving them. 2. Intentions provide a “filter” for adopting other intentions, which must not conflict. 5', 'INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 3. Agents track the success of their intentions and are inclined to try again if their attempts fail. 4. Agents believe their intentions are possible. 6', 'IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • 𝒫{Bel} is the power set of beliefs • Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have PROBLEM: • deliberation and means-ends reasoning processes are not instantaneous \uf0df Cost • So the agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world • The world may change in the meantime 11', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Explain how means-ends reasoning fit into the basic agent control loop • Representation • Relate blocksworld to the planning problem • Practical reasoning agent implementation • Deliberation 2', 'John McCarthy argued that there are occasions when the intentional stance is appropriate “We can ascribe beliefs, intentions, or desires to a machine when it helps us understand or explain its behaviour. Such descriptions are useful, even if the machine doesn’t literally “think” or “want. ” The goal is to predict, repair, or improve the system by reasoning about its internal state. Mental-like terms can simplify how we talk about complex systems — e. g.']","Based on the context provided, intentions play several distinct and critical roles in practical reasoning, which is a problem-solving process for agents.
+
+Here is an analysis of their specific roles based on the lecture content:
+
+### 1. Problem Solving and Goal Orientation
+Intents act as **tools** to solve problems rather than just sources of information.
+*   **Explanation**: The text states that ""Intents pose problems for agents."" In practical reasoning, an agent does not just *know* intentions; they must use them to navigate the world and achieve specific goals (e.g., ""I want to find a library"" vs. ""I want to eat an apple""). Without the ability to use intentions as a problem-solving mechanism, the agent would remain stuck in a loop of failure or inaction.
+
+### 2. The ""Filter"" for Conflict Resolution
+Intents are described as a **""filter""** for adopting other intentions.
+*   **Explanation**: When multiple actions or goals are being pursued, the agent must ensure they do not conflict. This aligns with the concept of **consistency** in practical reasoning. If Agent A intends to build a house, but Agent B intends to build a house, the agent must decide which intention to follow or pass both to avoid a conflict. Intentions serve as the boundary condition for decision-making.
+
+### 3. Success Tracking and Refinement
+Intention is linked to tracking the **success** of attempts and an inclination to try again if failures occur.
+*   **Explanation**: This aligns with **deliberation** and **means-end reasoning**. The agent observes the world, attempts a specific action (intention), and observes the result. If the outcome fails, the agent realizes the specific mistake or lack of motivation occurred at that moment. This leads to a self-correction loop where the goal is improved by adjusting future actions based on past observations.
+
+### 4. Agency and Belief Formation
+Intention is a **""power set of beliefs""** and is often **believed to be possible**.
+*   **Explanation**: This highlights how intentions function as a **belief system**. The agent possesses a set of possibilities (beliefs) about what *could* happen, and their current state of mind is directed toward those possibilities. The text also notes the agent believes these intentions are possible, meaning the agent has the mental capacity to act on them. This reduces cognitive load and allows for autonomous decision-making.
+
+### 5. Structural Alignment in Planning
+The integration of these concepts fits into a broader **Planning Problem** framework.
+*   **Explanation**: In the ""Planning"" section, the lecture explains how means-end reasoning fits into the agent's control loop. The agent selects an intention to achieve a result (means-end), tracks the result (success), and adapts (means-end) if necessary. This connects the abstract notion of ""intentions"" to concrete ""planning outcomes,"" ensuring the agent's actions are coordinated with the desired result.
+
+### Summary Conclusion
+In practical reasoning, intentions are the **decision-making driver**, the **safety net** against conflict, the **feedback mechanism**, and the **capacity for agency**. They transform passive observation of the world into active problem-solving by providing the criteria to evaluate actions, resolve conflicts, and learn from outcomes.","Intention poses problems for agents, who need to determine ways of achieving them. Intention provides a 'filter' for adopting other intentions which must not conflict.",0.25,0.959912131732464,0.36666666664833336,1.0
+What does the 'side effect or package deal problem' refer to in the context of practical reasoning?,"['INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 7. Agents need not intend all the expected side effects of their intentions. I may believe that going to the dentist involves pain, and I may also intend to go to the dentist — but this does not imply that I intend to suffer pain! 8 This is known as the side effect or package deal problem.', 'WHAT IS PRACTICAL REASONING? • Practical reasoning is reasoning directed towards actions — the process of figuring out what to do • Practical reasoning is a matter of weighing conflicting considerations for and against competing options, where the relevant considerations are provided by what the agent desires/values/cares about and what the agent believes (Bratman) • Distinguish practical reasoning from theoretical reasoning – directed towards belief 3', 'IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • 𝒫{Bel} is the power set of beliefs • Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have PROBLEM: • deliberation and means-ends reasoning processes are not instantaneous \uf0df Cost • So the agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world • The world may change in the meantime 11', 'LEARNING OUTCOMES At the end of this lecture, you should be able to: • Explain how means-ends reasoning fit into the basic agent control loop • Representation • Relate blocksworld to the planning problem • Practical reasoning agent implementation • Deliberation 2', 'NON-REQUESTED COORDINATION 6 Action equality: We both plan to do something, and by recognising this one of us can be saved the effort. Consequence: What I plan to do will have the side-effect of achieving something you want to do. Favor: What I plan to do will make it easier for you to do what you want to do.']","The ""side effect or package deal problem"" refers to the phenomenon where an agent's intention may not align with the expected outcome of performing those actions. In practical reasoning, this means that even if an agent believes an action (such as going to the dentist) will result in a desired outcome (like avoiding pain), they may still intend to perform the action. However, the actual outcome (pain) may not be the intended one.
+
+This problem highlights the importance of considering the agent's actual values, beliefs, and desires, which might not fully match the consequences of an action. For instance, an agent might strongly want to avoid pain but still intend to undergo a dental procedure because they value the convenience or the procedure itself. Practical reasoning involves weighing conflicting considerations, including the agent's desires, values, and beliefs, in order to determine the most appropriate course of action.
+
+The context provided in the questions and materials suggests that practical reasoning is concerned with evaluating the potential outcomes of actions based on the agent's understanding of what they desire or value. When an agent's intention and the resulting outcome may differ, practical reasoning helps to navigate these complexities by considering the agent's actual values and beliefs, rather than relying solely on their subjective desires.
+
+In summary, the ""side effect or package deal problem"" is a concept in practical reasoning that addresses the discrepancy between an agent's intention and the actual outcome. It emphasizes the need for a broader understanding of what the agent values, which may not always align with their desires, in order to make informed decisions.","The side effect or package deal problem refers to the fact that I may believe that going to the dentist involves pain, and I may also intend to go to the dentist — but this does not imply that I intend to suffer pain!",1.0,0.8412827086184101,0.9999999999,1.0
+What is the goal in the blocks world problem?,"['BLOCKSWORLD • Let’s look at the planning problem with reference to the blocks world. • The blocks world contains: • a robot arm, • 3 blocks (A, B and C) of equal size • a table-top • The aim is to generate a plan for the robot arm to build towers out of blocks 5', 'SUMMARY • We have looked at how goal, state, action and plan can be represented using logic. • We have used the blocksworld problem as an example • We have looked at reasoning agent implementation • We discussed deliberation and decomposed it to option generation and filtering • Next → Commitment 14', 'TWO ISSUES 10 The Transduction Problem The transduction problem is that of translating the real world into an accurate, adequate symbolic description, in time for that description to be useful. This has led onto research into vision, speech understanding, learning… The Representation / Reasoning Problem How to symbolically represent information about complex real-world entities and processes, and how to get agents to reason with this information in time for the results to be useful.', 'RECOGNITION • In this stage, an agent recognises it has a problem it needs help with • Agent has a goal, and either • realises it cannot achieve the goal in isolation - i.e. it does not have capability • realises it would prefer not to achieve the goal in isolation (typically because of solution quality, deadline etc.) • As a result, it needs to involve other agents 6', 'into one of the goal states g ∈ G 49 • A maintenance goal is specified by a set B of “bad” states: B ⊆ E • The agent succeeds in a particular environment if it manages to avoid all states in B — if it never performs actions which result in any state in B occurring • In terms of games, the agent succeeds in a maintenance task if it ensures that it is never forced into one of the fail states b ∈ B']","Based on the provided text, the goal in the blocks world problem is defined by the following key phrases:
