+What is the role of the Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS) in planning?,"['REPRESENTATION } 4 • goal to be achieved • state of environment • actions available to agent • plan itself Represented by logic The Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS): an automated planner used by Shakey Use first-order logic and theorem proving to plan strategies from start to goal', 'COOPERATIVE DISTRIBUTED PROBLEM SOLVING (CDPS) • CDPS studies how a loosely coupled network of problem solvers can work together to solve problems that are beyond their individual capabilities • Problems faced by the nodes cannot be completed without cooperation • No single node has sufficient expertise, resources, and information to solve a problem, and different nodes might have expertise solving different parts of the problem 6', 'and how to get agents to reason with this information in time for the results to be useful. This has led onto research into knowledge representation, automated reasoning, planning…', 'COOPERATIVE DISTRIBUTED PROBLEM SOLVING (CDPS) CPDS addresses the following: 1. Problem decomposition: How can a problem be divided into smaller tasks for distribution amongst agents? 2. Sub-problem solution: • How can the overall problem-solving activities of the agents be optimised so as to produce a solution that maximises the coherence metric? • What techniques can be used to coordinate the activity of the agents, thus avoiding destructive interactions? 3. Solution synthesis:', 'MEANS-ENDS REASONING / PLANNING • Planning: The design of a course of action that will achieve some desired goal • Basically, give a planning system representation of: • goal/intention to achieve; • actions it can perform; • the environment; • and generate a plan to achieve the goal 3 Let the system figure out how to achieve the goal on its own!']","Based on the context and the definitions provided in the PDFs, here is the analysis of the role of the Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS) in planning:
+
+### Logical Deduction and Analysis
+
+1.  **Context Identification**:
+    The text explicitly defines the STRIPS as an **""automated planner used by Shakey""** and mentions it is part of the **""Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver""** framework.
+
+2.  **Definition of a Planner**:
+    According to **[S5]**, a planner is a ""system of representation of: goal/intention, actions, environment, and generates a plan to achieve the goal.""
+
+3.  **Relationship between STRIPS and Planner**:
+    The text states: *""The Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS): an automated planner...""*. This establishes a direct functional relationship: STRIPS is the specific implementation of a planner within its domain.
+
+4.  **Function of STRIPS (as per [S2] and [S4])**:
+    *   **Work on a Loosely Coupled Network**: As described in **[S2]**, CDPS involves nodes that cannot solve problems independently. STRIPS is designed specifically to manage these ""loose coupling"" by coordinating the actions of the agents to solve the problem.
+    *   **Optimization (Sub-problem solution)**: **[S4]** discusses optimizing the ""overall problem-solving activities"" to achieve a ""solution that maximizes the coherence metric."" This implies that STRIPS must generate a strategy that optimizes the interaction among agents (e.g., timing, resource allocation, avoiding conflicts) to reach the desired state.
+
+### Conclusion
+
+The role of the **Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS)** is to act as the **automated planner**. It is responsible for generating and executing a specific plan (a sequence of actions) from an initial state to reach a target goal.
+
+In the context of **Cooperative Distributed Problem Solving (CDPS)** mentioned in **[S2]**, STRIPS plays a **core coordinating role**. It is the system that integrates the capabilities of the individual problem solvers (agents) to distribute tasks, coordinate their efforts, and ensure they work together to solve the problem successfully without individual failure, thereby fulfilling the requirement for **problem decomposition** and **solution synthesis**.
+
+**Summary Answer:**
+The **STRIPS** serves as the **automated planner** within the **Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver** framework. It is designed to take a loosely coupled network of agents, break the problem into smaller tasks, coordinate their activities (as described in **[S2]** and **[S4]**) to achieve the goal, and produce a plan to do so.",Use first-order logic and theorem proving to plan strategies from start to goal.,1.0,0.9999999999999473,0.8041666666465626,0.5
+How does an agent select an intention to achieve after observing the world?,"['IMPLEMENTING PRACTICAL REASONING AGENTS • 𝒫{Bel} is the power set of beliefs • Bel is the set of all possible beliefs that an agent might have PROBLEM: • deliberation and means-ends reasoning processes are not instantaneous \uf0df Cost • So the agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world • The world may change in the meantime 11', 'INTENTION RECONSIDERATION • An agent that does not stop to reconsider its intentions sufficiently often will continue to attempt to achieve its intentions even after it is clear that they cannot be achieved, or that there is no longer any reason for achieving them. • An agent that constantly reconsiders its attentions may spend insufficient time actually working to achieve them, and hence runs the risk of never actually achieving them. 9', 'OPTIMAL INTENTION RECONSIDERATION • Kinny and Georgeff’s experimentally investigated effectiveness of intention reconsideration strategies • Two different types of reconsideration strategy were used: • bold agents: never pause to reconsider intentions, and • cautious agents: stop to reconsider after every action. • Dynamism in the environment is represented by the rate of world change, γ. • Experiments were carried out using Tileword. 11', 'INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 3. Agents track the success of their intentions and are inclined to try again if their attempts fail. 4. Agents believe their intentions are possible. 6', 'INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 1. Intentions pose problems for agents, who need to determine ways of achieving them. 2. Intentions provide a “filter” for adopting other intentions, which must not conflict. 5']","An agent selects an intention to achieve after observing the world by using **deliberation and means-end reasoning processes**.
+
+In the context provided, an agent is not making a decision in the traditional sense (like choosing an action based on current information), but rather selecting an **intention** that has the potential to succeed. The process involves evaluating the current state of the world (its size, complexity, and changes) to decide what intentions are viable and likely to be achieved.
+
+### Key Mechanisms Involved:
+1.  **Observation of the World (Means-ends reasoning):** The agent examines the current state of affairs. If a specific intention is impossible given the current environment, it is discarded immediately.
+2.  **Goal Evaluation:** The agent assesses whether the goal of the intention is worth pursuing. For example, if an agent wants to complete a task but the task is impossible to start because the environment has changed drastically, the agent realizes the intention has failed.
+3.  **Selection Criterion:** The agent chooses the intention that offers the best probability of success. This is often based on a ""filter"" for adopting other intentions, meaning the agent rejects conflicting goals.
+
+### Comparison with Other Concepts:
+*   **Deliberation vs. Immediate Decision:** Unlike a typical decision-maker who halts and chooses an action, an agent acts on the *existence* of an intention. They may delay (reconsideration) if they can confirm the success, but the selection of the ""intention"" itself happens during the deliberative phase.
+*   **Cost and Risk:** Since deliberation is not instantaneous, the agent must weigh the risks and costs of pursuing different intentions to ensure the chosen one is optimal.
+
+**Conclusion:** The selection of the intention is an act of reasoned deliberation where the agent evaluates the feasibility and probability of the goal based on the immediate state of the world, rather than executing a specific action or making a final choice.",The agent selects an intention to achieve that would have been optimal at the time it observed the world.,0.9285714285714286,0.9643777134407885,0.9999999999666667,1.0
+What does the filter function in option generation and filtering do?,"['OPTION GENERATION AND FILTERING OPTION GENERATION FILTERING The agent generates a set of possible alternatives The agent chooses between competing alternatives, and commits to achieving them Represent option generation via a function, options(), which takes the agent’s current beliefs and current intentions, and from them determines a set of options In order to select between competing options, an agent uses a filter() function Desires Intentions options : 𝒫(Bel) × 𝒫(Int) ➝ 𝒫(Des) filter :', 'DELIBERATION • How does an agent deliberate? • begin by trying to understand what the options available to you are • choose between them and commit to some • Chosen options are then intentions • The deliberate function can be decomposed into two distinct functional components: • option generation • filtering 12', 'LAYERED APPROACH - VERTICAL Vertical layering: • Only one layer connected to sensors/effectors • Filtering approach (one-pass control): propagate intermediate decisions from one layer to another • Abstraction layer approach (two- pass control): different layers make decisions at different levels of abstraction 6', 'ACTION FUNCTION 15', 'INTENTIONS IN PRACTICAL REASONING 1. Intentions pose problems for agents, who need to determine ways of achieving them. 2. Intentions provide a “filter” for adopting other intentions, which must not conflict. 5']","The filter function in option generation and filtering is designed to **select or reject** between competing alternatives based on the agent's current beliefs and intentions.
+
+### How it works:
+1. **Inputs**: The filter function takes two sets of beliefs:
+   - **Beliefs (𝒫(Bel))**: The agent's current knowledge or perspective.
+   - **Intents (𝒫(Int))**: The agent's current intentions or goals.
+
+2. **Process**: The filter evaluates the compatibility of the agent's beliefs and intentions to determine whether they align.
+
+3. **Output**: The filter returns a set of options (𝒫(Des)) that are viable or required, excluding conflicting beliefs or intentions.
+
+### Key Purpose:
+- It ensures that the agent adheres to its intentions by filtering out options that would undermine those intentions.
+- It also helps prioritize or select specific alternatives that align with the agent's current state of knowledge and goals.
+
+In essence, the filter function acts as a mechanism for **rational decision-making**, ensuring that the agent's actions are consistent with its intentions and available information.","In order to select between competing options, an agent uses a filter() function.",1.0,0.9794199608864718,0.99999999998,1.0
+What is the representation of the goal in the STRIPS planning system?,"['REPRESENTATION } 4 • goal to be achieved • state of environment • actions available to agent • plan itself Represented by logic The Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver (STRIPS): an automated planner used by Shakey Use first-order logic and theorem proving to plan strategies from start to goal', 'MEANS-ENDS REASONING / PLANNING • Planning: The design of a course of action that will achieve some desired goal • Basically, give a planning system representation of: • goal/intention to achieve; • actions it can perform; • the environment; • and generate a plan to achieve the goal 3 Let the system figure out how to achieve the goal on its own!', 'TOURINGMACHINES • Control sub-system consists of • Reactive layer: situation-action rules • Planning layer: construction of plans and action selection • Modelling layer: contains symbolic representations of mental states of other agents • The three layers communicate via explicit control rules 8', 'IMPLEMENTED BDI AGENTS: PROCEDURAL REASONING SYSTEM • In the PRS, each agent is equipped with a plan library, representing that agent’s procedural knowledge: knowledge about the mechanisms that can be used by the agent in order to realise its intentions. • The options available to an agent are directly determined by the plans an agent has: an agent with no plans has no options 13 PRS agents have explicit representations of beliefs, desires, and intentions, as above', 'MAJA MATARIC’S TOTO • Can map spaces and execute plans without the need for a symbolic representation • Each feature/landmark is a set of sensor readings • Recorded in a behaviour as a triple: • Landmark type • Compass heading • Approximate length/size 3']","The goal in the STRIPS planning system is represented by a **state** within the logical representation of the planning system. Specifically, the goal is defined as the final state of the environment where the planning process has successfully completed.
