+What is the main reason for needing agent-oriented methodologies?,"The main reason for needing agent-oriented methodologies is to address mismatches that arise when traditional object-oriented (OO) methodologies are applied to multi-agent systems. These mismatches include concepts like classes and objects, which are not directly applicable to the distributed and autonomous nature of agents.
+
+- **Sources**: [S1], [S2], [S3]","['AGENT ORIENTED METHODOLOGIES •There is need for agent -oriented methodologies → centred around specific agent -oriented abstractions •The adoption of OO methodologies would produce mismatches - Classes, objects, client -servers: little to do with agents 3'
+ 'SUMMARY •In this lecture, we explored why traditional object -oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi -agent systems. •We introduced agent -oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. •We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent -based analysis and design. •We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23'
+ 'AGENT ORIENTED METHODOLOGIES •Each methodology may introduce further abstractions - around which to model software and to organize the software process •For example: roles, organizations, responsibilities, belief, desire and intentions •Not directly translating into concrete entities of the software system → the concept of role is an aspect of an agent , not an agent 4'
+ 'AGENT -ORIENTED PROGRAMMING •Yoav Shoham introduced “agent -oriented programming” in 1990: “... new programming paradigm, based on a societal view of computation ...” •The key idea: •Directly programming agents in terms of intentional notions •like belief, desire, and intention •Adopts the same abstraction as humans •Resulted in the Agent0 programming language 21'
+ 'RELEVANT METHODOLOGIES •GAIA Methodologies •Encourages a developer to think of building agent -based systems as a process of organisational design •Agent UML •Extend Unified Modeling Language (UML) with agent features •Prometeus •Focuses mainly on the internal agent architecture → basically a methodology for designing BDI agent systems 7']",There is need for agent-oriented methodologies → centred around specific agent-oriented abstractions,0.0,0.9999999999998549,0.99999999998,0.3
+What does the adoption of Object-Oriented (OO) methodologies produce?,"The adoption of Object-Oriented (OO) methodologies produces mismatches when applied to multi-agent systems, as traditional OO constructs like classes and objects do not directly align with the nature of agents and their interactions.
+
+- **Explanation:**
+- [S1] states that the adoption of OO methodologies for multi-agent systems leads to mismatches. Specifically, it mentions that classes and objects, along with client-server models, do not easily fit the requirements of agents, which often involve roles, interactions, and coordination. The mismatch arises because traditional OO constructs are not designed to handle the decentralized and autonomous nature of agents.
+- [S3] further elaborates on this point, indicating that while some agent-oriented methodologies introduce new abstractions like roles, these abstractions often don't directly translate into concrete entities within the software system. Instead, the concept of a role is more about an aspect of an agent rather than a concrete entity.
+- [S2] provides a more comprehensive overview, highlighting that traditional OO methodologies are not sufficient for multi-agent systems, necessitating the development of agent-oriented methodologies focusing on roles, interactions, and coordination.
+
+**Sources: [S1], [S3], [S2]","['AGENT ORIENTED METHODOLOGIES •There is need for agent -oriented methodologies → centred around specific agent -oriented abstractions •The adoption of OO methodologies would produce mismatches - Classes, objects, client -servers: little to do with agents 3'
+ 'SUMMARY •In this lecture, we explored why traditional object -oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi -agent systems. •We introduced agent -oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. •We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent -based analysis and design. •We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23'
+ 'AGENT ORIENTED METHODOLOGIES •Each methodology may introduce further abstractions - around which to model software and to organize the software process •For example: roles, organizations, responsibilities, belief, desire and intentions •Not directly translating into concrete entities of the software system → the concept of role is an aspect of an agent , not an agent 4'
+ 'RESULT SHARING IN SUBSCRIBE/NOTIFY PATTERN •Common design pattern in OO systems: subscribe/notify •An object subscribes to another object, saying “tell me when event e happens” •When event e happens, original object is notified •Information pro -actively shared between objects •Objects required to know about the interests of other objects → inform objects when relevant information arises 9'
+ 'HUMAN ORIENTATION •The movement away from machine - oriented views of programming toward concepts and metaphors that more closely reflect the way we ourselves understand the world •Programmers conceptualize and implement software in terms of ever higher -level – more human -oriented – abstractions 16']","The adoption of OO methodologies would produce mismatches - Classes, objects, client-servers: little to do with agents",1.0,0.6233372248342323,0.99999999998,nan
+What are the main differences between Agent-Based Analysis and Agent-Based Design?,"Agent-Based Analysis focuses on understanding the main actors and their interactions with the system, whereas Agent-Based Design focuses on the engineering aspects such as component interactions and responsibilities. Analysis aims to identify roles, responsibilities, and capabilities, while design aims to engineer these components and their interactions within the system.
+
+**Sources**: [S1], [S2]","['AGENT -BASED ANALYSIS •Analysis aims to understand , at least •What are the main actors interacting with the system •How the system interacts with these actors •What the system is supposed to do •Then, we associate accordingly •Roles responsibilities and capabilities •Interaction patterns between agents 5'
+ 'AGENT -BASED DESIGN •Design aims to engineer , at least •What are the main components interacting within the system •What are the responsibilities and the capabilities of each component in the system •How the components interact to implement the system, i.e., the architecture of the system •Differently from analysis: we need to choose on which agents to use and how they interact 6'
+ 'SUMMARY •In this lecture, we explored why traditional object -oriented methodologies are not sufficient for building multi -agent systems. •We introduced agent -oriented methodologies that focus on roles, interactions, and coordination between agents. •We discussed examples such as GAIA, Agent UML, and Prometheus to illustrate different approaches to agent -based analysis and design. •We highlighted the importance of separating agent analysis from agent design to manage system complexity. 23'
+ 'RELEVANT METHODOLOGIES •GAIA Methodologies •Encourages a developer to think of building agent -based systems as a process of organisational design •Agent UML •Extend Unified Modeling Language (UML) with agent features •Prometeus •Focuses mainly on the internal agent architecture → basically a methodology for designing BDI agent systems 7'
+ 'WHAT IS AN AGENT? •The main point about agents is they are autonomous : capable independent action 4An agent is a computer system that is situated in some environment, and that is capable of autonomous action in that environment in order to meet its delegated objectives •It is all about decisions: •An agent must choose what action to perform •An agent must decide when to perform an action']","Agent-based analysis aims to understand, at least, what are the main actors interacting with the system and how the system interacts with these actors. Agent-based design aims to engineer, at least, what are the main components interacting within the system and what are the responsibilities and capabilities of each component in the system. Differently from analysis: we need to choose on which agents to use and how they interact.",1.0,0.9999999999998911,0.99999999998,nan
